var e={level:`C2`,type:`true-false`,questions:[{id:`c2-tf-001`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says critics 'have long debated', early books were 'praised for their lyrical prose', and restraint 'lends the later novels their quiet power'.`,passage:`Critics have long debated whether the novelist's later work marks a decline or a deliberate shift in style. Her early books, praised for their lyrical prose, gave way to spare, almost austere narratives. Defenders argue that this restraint is precisely what lends the later novels their quiet power.`,statements:[{text:`All critics agree that her later work is weaker.`,answer:!1},{text:`Her early books were praised for their lyrical prose.`,answer:!0},{text:`Defenders see the restraint as a strength.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-002`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says urban noise has troubled people 'for centuries' and medieval towns 'passed laws restricting hammering'.`,passage:`Far from being a purely modern problem, urban noise has troubled city dwellers for centuries. Ancient Roman writers complained about the clatter of carts at night, and medieval towns passed laws restricting hammering after dark.`,statements:[{text:`Urban noise is a recent problem.`,answer:!1},{text:`Medieval towns tried to limit noise by law.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-003`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the brain was once thought to 'switch off', memories are 'reorganised as they are stored', some nap studies 'have proved difficult to repeat', and few scientists dispute that lack of sleep impairs learning.`,passage:`For much of the twentieth century, sleep was regarded as little more than a period of rest during which the brain more or less switched off. That assumption has since been overturned. Researchers now argue that the sleeping brain is remarkably busy, sorting through the experiences of the day and deciding which to keep. During deep sleep, it appears, recently formed memories are replayed and gradually transferred to longer-term storage, while less useful information is allowed to fade. The process is not a simple recording, however. Memories seem to be reorganised as they are stored, which may explain why people sometimes wake with a fresh solution to a problem that baffled them the night before. Not every claim in this field is settled. Some studies that reported dramatic improvements in learning after a nap have proved difficult to repeat, and critics warn against overstating results from small groups of volunteers. What few scientists now dispute is that a persistent lack of sleep impairs both concentration and the ability to learn, a finding with obvious implications for students who sacrifice rest in order to revise.`,statements:[{text:`Scientists in the twentieth century generally saw sleep as an active period for the brain.`,answer:!1},{text:`Memories are stored during sleep in exactly the form in which they were experienced.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some research results on napping have been hard to reproduce.`,answer:!0},{text:`There is broad agreement that long-term lack of sleep harms learning.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-004`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text mentions companies 'eager to display their environmental credentials', says honeybee numbers are 'in no danger of collapse', that most wild bees 'live alone', and that researchers suggest cities 'cap the number of hives'.`,passage:`Rooftop hives have become a familiar sight in many European cities, promoted by companies eager to display their environmental credentials. At first glance, the trend seems wholly positive: honeybees are in decline in parts of the countryside, so giving them a home among office blocks sounds like a sensible remedy. Ecologists, however, are increasingly sceptical. The honeybee is a managed, domesticated species, and its numbers are in no danger of collapse. The insects that are genuinely under threat are the hundreds of species of wild bee, most of which live alone rather than in colonies. When too many hives are placed in a small area, honeybees compete with these wild species for a limited supply of flowers, and may even spread disease to them. Some researchers therefore suggest that cities should cap the number of hives and invest instead in planting a wider variety of flowering plants, which benefit all pollinators. A rooftop hive, they argue, can easily become a symbol of concern for nature rather than a meaningful contribution to protecting it.`,statements:[{text:`Some companies keep rooftop hives partly to improve their public image.`,answer:!0},{text:`Ecologists believe that honeybees are at serious risk of disappearing.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most species of wild bee live in large colonies.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some researchers propose limiting the number of hives in cities.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-005`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the difference 'hardly mattered' before trains, towns changed 'not without resistance', some clocks 'displayed two minute hands', and standard time became law only in 1880, after the railways.`,passage:`Before the arrival of the railways, every town in Britain kept its own time, set by the position of the sun. Noon in Bristol fell roughly ten minutes later than noon in London, a difference that hardly mattered when a journey between the two took several days. Fast trains changed that. Timetables became almost impossible to read when each station followed its own clock, and the risk of collisions on single-track lines made confusion potentially deadly. One by one, the railway companies adopted London time for their operations, and many towns soon followed, though not without resistance. Some local authorities saw the change as an attack on their independence, and for years a number of public clocks displayed two minute hands, one showing local time and one showing railway time. It was not until 1880 that a single standard time became law across the whole of Great Britain. By then, of course, most people had long since stopped caring what the sun was doing at midday.`,statements:[{text:`Differences in local time caused few problems before the railways.`,answer:!0},{text:`Every town accepted railway time immediately.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some public clocks showed two different times.`,answer:!0},{text:`A single national time became law before the first railways were built.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-006`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says open offices were 'sold' to encourage collaboration, face-to-face interaction 'fell by around two thirds', the open office is not 'doomed', and it is 'considerably cheaper' than private rooms.`,passage:`Open-plan offices were sold to employers as a way to encourage spontaneous conversation and collaboration. Remove the walls, the argument went, and ideas would flow freely between colleagues who would otherwise never speak. The evidence for this claim has turned out to be surprisingly thin. One frequently cited study, which tracked employees before and after their company moved to an open layout, found that face-to-face interaction actually fell by around two thirds, while the number of emails rose sharply. Faced with constant noise and the feeling of being watched, workers seemed to retreat behind headphones and screens. None of this means that the open office is doomed. Its defenders point out that it is considerably cheaper than giving everyone a private room, and that some teams genuinely thrive on the buzz of a shared space. A growing number of firms now favour a compromise: open areas for group work, combined with quiet rooms that anyone can book when they need to concentrate. Whether this hybrid will deliver the promised creativity remains to be seen.`,statements:[{text:`Open-plan offices were promoted as a way to increase collaboration.`,answer:!0},{text:`The study found that employees talked to each other more after the move.`,answer:!1},{text:`The writer believes that open-plan offices will soon disappear.`,answer:!1},{text:`Open offices cost less than giving every employee a private room.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-007`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says a strong placebo response can make a useful medicine 'look ineffective', expensive-looking pills work better, patients can benefit when 'told openly' the pill is sugar, and placebos do not 'heal broken bones'.`,passage:`The placebo effect, in which patients improve after receiving a treatment with no active ingredient, has long been treated as a nuisance by medical researchers. New drugs must outperform a dummy pill in clinical trials, and a strong placebo response can make a genuinely useful medicine look ineffective. Yet a number of scientists have begun to view the phenomenon as worthy of study in its own right. Their experiments suggest that the context of treatment matters a great deal: expensive-looking pills tend to work better than cheap ones, and injections tend to outperform tablets, even when all of them are inactive. More surprisingly, some trials have found that patients can benefit even when they are told openly that the pill they are taking contains nothing but sugar. The effect is not unlimited. Placebos do not shrink tumours or heal broken bones, and their influence appears strongest on symptoms such as pain and fatigue, which are shaped partly by the way the brain interprets signals from the body. Understanding why may help doctors make better use of the treatments that do work.`,statements:[{text:`A strong placebo response can make it harder to show that a new drug works.`,answer:!0},{text:`Cheap-looking pills usually produce a stronger placebo effect.`,answer:!1},{text:`In some trials, patients improved even though they knew the pill was inactive.`,answer:!0},{text:`Placebos have been shown to heal serious physical injuries.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-008`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the old kilogram was a cylinder, not a law of nature; copies drifted by about 'fifty micrograms'; the public hardly noticed the change; and the cylinder 'still exists'.`,passage:`For more than a century, the kilogram was defined not by a law of nature but by a small cylinder of platinum and iridium locked in a vault near Paris. Every other mass standard in the world was ultimately calibrated against this single object, which scientists handled with almost religious care. The arrangement had an obvious weakness: if the cylinder gained or lost even a few atoms, the definition of the kilogram itself would shift, and nobody could say with confidence which had changed, the prototype or its copies. Periodic comparisons revealed precisely such discrepancies, amounting to some fifty micrograms over several decades. In 2019, after years of painstaking experiments, the international scientific community redefined the unit in terms of the Planck constant, a fixed value that underpins quantum physics. The change went largely unnoticed by the public, since a bag of flour weighs exactly what it did before. For metrologists, however, it marked the end of an era in which a fundamental unit depended on a physical artefact that could be scratched, contaminated or, in principle, stolen. The old cylinder still exists, but it is now merely a historical curiosity.`,statements:[{text:`Until 2019, the kilogram was based on a constant of nature.`,answer:!1},{text:`Comparisons showed small differences between the prototype and its copies.`,answer:!0},{text:`The redefinition changed the weight of everyday goods.`,answer:!1},{text:`The original cylinder was destroyed after the redefinition.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-009`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the fungi supply water and minerals and receive sugars; sceptics say the evidence for sharing is 'thin'; the networks' existence is 'beyond doubt'; and sceptics compare the forest to 'a marketplace'.`,passage:`Beneath the floor of almost every forest lies a dense web of fungal threads that wrap around tree roots and extend far into the soil. These partnerships, known as mycorrhizae, are ancient and widespread: the fungi supply trees with water and minerals, and in return receive sugars produced by photosynthesis. In recent years, the idea that such networks allow trees to share resources and even send warning signals to one another has captured the public imagination, inspiring books, documentaries and a good deal of poetic language about forests as cooperative communities. Some researchers, though, have grown uneasy. They point out that many of the most widely quoted findings come from a handful of small studies, and that the evidence for deliberate sharing between trees, as opposed to fungi simply moving nutrients for their own benefit, remains thin. None of this disputes the existence of the networks themselves, which is beyond doubt. The quarrel is over interpretation, and over how far scientific caution should yield to an appealing story. For now, the sceptics argue, the forest is better described as a marketplace than as a family.`,statements:[{text:`The fungi receive sugars from the trees.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics doubt that fungal networks exist at all.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some researchers think the evidence for trees deliberately sharing resources is weak.`,answer:!0},{text:`The sceptics describe the forest as a family.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-010`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says batteries contain 'valuable metals'; smelting 'loses most of the lithium'; chemical methods produce liquid waste; and some engineers think the breakthrough will come from 'better design'.`,passage:`As electric cars and portable devices multiply, so does a less glamorous problem: what to do with millions of exhausted lithium-ion batteries. Discarded carelessly, they pose a fire risk and can leach toxic substances into the ground. Yet they also contain valuable metals such as cobalt, nickel and lithium, whose extraction from mines carries a heavy environmental and human cost. Recycling would seem the obvious answer, but it has proved stubbornly difficult. Batteries come in a bewildering variety of shapes and chemistries, and few were designed with disassembly in mind. The traditional method, which involves smelting them at high temperatures, recovers some metals but loses most of the lithium and consumes vast amounts of energy. Newer techniques use chemical solutions to dissolve and separate the components, achieving far higher recovery rates, though they generate liquid waste that must itself be treated. Some engineers argue that the real breakthrough will come not from better recycling plants but from better design: batteries built in standard formats, clearly labelled and easy to take apart. Until then, much of the material will continue to end up in landfill or be shipped abroad.`,statements:[{text:`Used batteries contain metals worth recovering.`,answer:!0},{text:`Smelting recovers almost all of the lithium in a battery.`,answer:!1},{text:`The newer chemical methods produce no waste at all.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some engineers believe battery design matters more than recycling technology.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-011`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the advice is rarely questioned; longer exposure may 'increase the risk'; tuberculosis needs 'prolonged treatment'; and some doctors fear 'confusion'.`,passage:`Generations of patients have been told to finish a course of antibiotics even after they feel better, on the grounds that stopping early encourages resistant bacteria to survive. The advice sounds sensible, and it is repeated so often that few people question it. Some infectious disease specialists, however, now argue that it lacks a solid scientific basis. Resistance, they point out, develops largely because bacteria are exposed to antibiotics, so taking the drugs for longer than necessary may actually increase the risk rather than reduce it. For many common infections, recent trials suggest that shorter courses work just as well as the traditional ones. That is not to say patients should simply stop whenever they choose; for certain conditions, such as tuberculosis, prolonged treatment is essential, and stopping too soon can be dangerous. The specialists' point is rather that the length of a course ought to be determined by evidence for each illness, not by a one-size-fits-all slogan. Changing a message that has been drilled into the public for decades will not be easy, and some doctors fear that any mixed signals could lead to confusion.`,statements:[{text:`The advice to finish a course of antibiotics is rarely challenged by the public.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to the specialists, longer courses always reduce resistance.`,answer:!1},{text:`The specialists say patients with tuberculosis may stop treatment whenever they feel better.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some doctors worry that changing the advice could confuse people.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-012`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says historians tell 'a far less dramatic story'; Caesar's fire caused damage but no reliable evidence shows total destruction; decline was 'gradual'; and papyrus 'needed constant copying'.`,passage:`Few episodes in history have been so thoroughly romanticised as the destruction of the great library of Alexandria. In the popular imagination, a single catastrophic fire consumed the accumulated wisdom of the ancient world in one night, plunging humanity into centuries of ignorance. Historians tell a far less dramatic story. The library, founded under the Ptolemaic kings, certainly suffered damage when Julius Caesar's troops set fire to ships in the harbour, but there is no reliable evidence that it was wiped out on that occasion. Its decline appears instead to have been gradual, the result of dwindling royal funding, political purges that drove scholars away, and the slow decay of papyrus scrolls that needed constant copying to survive. By the time the last traces disappeared, the institution had long ceased to be the centre of learning it once was. The myth persists, some suggest, because a sudden act of violence makes a more satisfying tale than a slow loss of money and interest. Its lesson, if there is one, may be less about barbarians at the gate than about the quiet neglect of knowledge.`,statements:[{text:`Historians generally accept that a single fire destroyed the library overnight.`,answer:!1},{text:`The library was damaged when Caesar's soldiers set fire to ships.`,answer:!0},{text:`Reduced funding contributed to the library's decline.`,answer:!0},{text:`Papyrus scrolls could survive for centuries without being copied.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-013`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says critics thought the cleaning removed the artist's glazes; leaving varnish is 'hardly a neutral choice'; conservators favour reversible treatments; and technology cannot show exactly how the painting first looked.`,passage:`When restorers removed centuries of yellowed varnish from a celebrated altarpiece, visitors were astonished to find colours far brighter than anyone alive had ever seen. Not everyone was delighted. A vocal group of critics complained that the cleaning had stripped away subtle glazes applied by the artist himself, leaving the figures flat and garish. The dispute illustrates a dilemma that lies at the heart of conservation. Varnish darkens and cracks with age, obscuring detail and distorting colour, so leaving it in place is hardly a neutral choice. Yet removing it requires restorers to make judgements about which layers are original and which were added later, judgements that cannot always be verified. Modern conservators rely heavily on scientific analysis, such as infrared imaging and microscopic sampling, to guide their decisions, and they generally favour treatments that can be reversed if later generations disagree. Even so, no amount of technology can tell them precisely what a painting looked like on the day it was finished. Every restoration, in the end, reflects the taste and knowledge of its own time as much as the intentions of the artist.`,statements:[{text:`Some critics claimed the cleaning removed layers added by the original artist.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to the text, leaving old varnish in place has no effect on a painting.`,answer:!1},{text:`Conservators usually prefer treatments that can be undone.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientific analysis can reveal exactly how a painting looked when it was completed.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-014`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Wegener was 'met with ridicule'; he lacked 'a convincing mechanism'; he was a meteorologist; and plate tectonics differed from his model 'in important details'.`,passage:`When the German scientist Alfred Wegener proposed in 1912 that the continents had once been joined together and had since drifted apart, he was met with ridicule. His evidence was intriguing: the coastlines of Africa and South America fit together like pieces of a puzzle, and identical fossils had been found on landmasses separated by vast oceans. What he could not supply was a convincing mechanism. Leading geologists dismissed the idea that solid continents could plough through the ocean floor, and Wegener, a meteorologist by training, was easily portrayed as an outsider meddling in a field he did not understand. He died on an expedition to Greenland in 1930, his theory still widely rejected. Only in the 1950s and 1960s did new techniques for mapping the sea floor reveal ridges where fresh crust was forming and spreading outwards, providing the missing engine. The theory of plate tectonics, which grew out of these discoveries, vindicated much of what Wegener had argued, though it differed from his model in important details. His story is often cited as a warning against dismissing unconventional ideas too hastily.`,statements:[{text:`Wegener's ideas were warmly received when he first proposed them.`,answer:!1},{text:`His main difficulty was explaining how the continents could move.`,answer:!0},{text:`Wegener was trained as a geologist.`,answer:!1},{text:`Plate tectonics confirmed every detail of Wegener's original model.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-015`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says scraps supplement rather than dominate the diet; city foxes are not markedly larger; numbers in several cities 'have remained broadly stable'; and residents' feelings go 'in both directions'.`,passage:`Foxes have become such a familiar feature of many British cities that residents scarcely look up when one trots across the road at dusk. Their success is often attributed to the abundance of food waste, and there is some truth in this, but researchers who have tracked urban foxes paint a more complex picture. Much of their diet consists of earthworms, insects, fruit and small rodents, supplemented rather than dominated by scraps from bins and deliberate feeding by householders. Gardens, railway embankments and cemeteries provide shelter and safe places to raise cubs. Contrary to a common belief, city foxes are not markedly larger or more aggressive than their rural cousins, and attacks on people are extremely rare. Nor are urban populations growing without limit; in several cities, numbers have remained broadly stable for decades, held in check by disease, road traffic and competition for territory. The animals provoke strong feelings in both directions, with some residents delighted by their presence and others regarding them as pests. What seems clear is that foxes have adapted to the urban environment far more successfully than humans once imagined possible.`,statements:[{text:`Food from bins makes up most of an urban fox's diet.`,answer:!1},{text:`City foxes are not significantly bigger than rural foxes.`,answer:!0},{text:`In several cities, fox numbers have stayed roughly the same for many years.`,answer:!0},{text:`All residents welcome the presence of foxes.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-016`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says historians 'have questioned parts of this story'; the layout became entrenched after training and standardisation; no alternative has 'made significant headway'; and some say the alternatives' benefits are exaggerated.`,passage:`The arrangement of letters on a standard keyboard has puzzled generations of typists. Why should the most common letters be scattered so awkwardly, forcing the weaker fingers to do much of the work? The usual explanation is that the layout was devised for early typewriters, whose metal arms tended to jam when adjacent keys were struck in quick succession, and that separating frequent letter pairs reduced the problem. Historians have questioned parts of this story, noting that the design evolved through many revisions shaped by commercial pressures as much as by mechanics. Whatever its origins, the layout became entrenched once typists had been trained on it and manufacturers had standardised their machines. Alternative arrangements, some claiming substantial gains in speed and comfort, have been proposed repeatedly, yet none has made significant headway. Economists cite the keyboard as a classic example of path dependence, the way an early choice can lock in a technology long after the reasons for it have vanished. Some, however, argue that the advantages of the alternatives have been exaggerated, which would make the case less a story of inefficiency than of reasonable habit.`,statements:[{text:`Historians fully accept the traditional explanation for the keyboard layout.`,answer:!1},{text:`Training and standardisation helped the layout become fixed.`,answer:!0},{text:`Alternative layouts have largely replaced the standard one.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some experts doubt that the alternative layouts are much better.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-017`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says gut microbes were once seen as 'passengers'; diverse plant foods are linked to more diverse bacteria; many findings come from mice; and most supplements have little rigorous research.`,passage:`The trillions of microbes living in the human gut were once regarded as little more than passengers. Over the past two decades, however, they have become one of the most fashionable subjects in medical research, linked in various studies to obesity, allergies, mood and even the effectiveness of cancer treatments. Diet appears to play a major role in shaping this community. People who eat a wide variety of plant foods tend to host a more diverse range of bacteria, and diversity is generally associated with better health. Fibre, in particular, feeds species that produce compounds thought to reduce inflammation. Enthusiasm has outpaced evidence in some areas, though. Many of the most striking findings come from experiments on mice, whose digestive systems differ considerably from ours, and it is often unclear whether changes in gut bacteria cause disease or merely accompany it. Shops are now full of supplements promising to restore a healthy balance, but most have little rigorous research behind them. Scientists working in the field tend to offer more modest advice: eat plenty of vegetables, fruit and whole grains, and be wary of products that claim miraculous results.`,statements:[{text:`Gut bacteria have always been considered important to health.`,answer:!1},{text:`Eating many kinds of plant foods is linked to a more varied gut community.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some important findings are based on studies of animals.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most gut-health supplements are supported by strong scientific evidence.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-018`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says scribes worked 'by daylight'; margins contain complaints about cold and aching hands; scholars 'have long argued' about the images; and the notes reveal 'personalities'.`,passage:`Medieval manuscripts were produced with extraordinary patience. In monastic workshops, scribes spent months copying a single book by hand, working by daylight on parchment made from animal skins. Their task was meant to be one of devout concentration, yet the margins of surviving manuscripts suggest that boredom and discomfort were never far away. Scattered among the sacred texts are small notes of complaint: that the ink is thin, that the room is cold, that the writer's hand aches and he longs for his dinner. Even more surprising to modern readers are the illustrations that occasionally appear beside the text, in which knights fight snails, rabbits hunt hunters and monkeys play musical instruments. Scholars have long argued over the meaning of these images. Some see them as satire, mocking the pretensions of the powerful; others interpret them as moral warnings, or simply as the playful inventions of artists given a little freedom at the edge of the page. What is certain is that they offer a rare glimpse of the personalities behind works that were otherwise designed to be impersonal, and they remind us that the people who made them were very human.`,statements:[{text:`Scribes often worked through the night by candlelight.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some marginal notes complain about physical discomfort.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scholars agree that the strange illustrations were meant as satire.`,answer:!1},{text:`The marginal notes reveal something about the individuals who made the books.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-019`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says improvisation 'rests on years of disciplined preparation'; players often transcribe solos; the most admired improvisers listen most attentively; and critics say many trained players 'sound strangely alike'.`,passage:`To audiences unfamiliar with the tradition, jazz improvisation can seem like pure spontaneity, as if musicians were simply inventing whatever came into their heads. In reality, it rests on years of disciplined preparation. Players absorb the harmonic structure of hundreds of tunes, practise scales and patterns until they become second nature, and study recordings of earlier masters, often transcribing solos note by note. This vocabulary allows them, in performance, to respond instantly to what their fellow musicians are doing, much as a fluent speaker can join a conversation without rehearsing each sentence. Far from limiting creativity, such preparation is what makes genuine invention possible. The most admired improvisers are not those who play the most notes, but those who listen most attentively, leaving space for others and building on ideas that emerge within the group. Some critics complain that jazz education, now largely based in universities, has produced technically brilliant players who sound strangely alike. Others reply that the tradition has always balanced imitation and originality, and that every great innovator began by learning to play like somebody else.`,statements:[{text:`Jazz improvisation requires little or no preparation.`,answer:!1},{text:`Musicians often copy down earlier solos in detail.`,answer:!0},{text:`The best improvisers are those who play the greatest number of notes.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some critics believe university-trained players lack individuality.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-020`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says outer stars orbit 'far faster' than expected; dark matter outweighs ordinary matter about 'five to one'; no particle has been detected directly; and modified theories 'struggle with other observations'.`,passage:`Astronomers have known for decades that something is wrong with the way galaxies move. Stars on the outer edges of spiral galaxies orbit far faster than they should if the only mass present were the matter we can see, and clusters of galaxies hold together more tightly than visible matter alone could explain. The most widely accepted solution is that the universe contains vast quantities of dark matter, an invisible substance that exerts gravity but neither emits nor absorbs light. Estimates suggest it outweighs ordinary matter by roughly five to one. Yet despite enormous effort, including sensitive detectors buried deep underground and experiments at particle colliders, no one has managed to detect a single particle of it directly. This persistent failure has encouraged a minority of physicists to explore a radical alternative: that our understanding of gravity itself breaks down at very large scales. Such modified theories can explain the motion of individual galaxies remarkably well, but they struggle with other observations, such as patterns in the radiation left over from the early universe. For the time being, dark matter remains the leading explanation, though an unproven one.`,statements:[{text:`Stars at the edges of spiral galaxies move more slowly than expected.`,answer:!1},{text:`Dark matter is thought to be more abundant than ordinary matter.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists have detected dark matter particles in underground laboratories.`,answer:!1},{text:`Modified theories of gravity cannot easily explain every observation.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-021`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says bleached corals 'are not necessarily dead'; the algae live inside coral tissues; intervals between events 'have been shrinking'; and interventions can only 'buy time'.`,passage:`Coral reefs cover only a tiny fraction of the ocean floor, yet they support roughly a quarter of all marine species. Their survival depends on a delicate partnership between coral animals and microscopic algae that live inside their tissues, providing food through photosynthesis and giving reefs their vivid colours. When the water becomes too warm for too long, this partnership breaks down. The stressed corals expel the algae, turning ghostly white in a process known as bleaching. Bleached corals are not necessarily dead; if temperatures return to normal quickly enough, the algae can recolonise them and the reef may recover. Repeated episodes, however, leave little time for recovery, and the intervals between major bleaching events have been shrinking as the oceans warm. Scientists are experimenting with ways to help, from breeding heat-tolerant corals to shading reefs and transplanting fragments grown in nurseries. Most researchers stress that such measures can buy time at best. Without substantial cuts in greenhouse gas emissions, they warn, even the most ingenious interventions will be overwhelmed, and many of the reefs we know today may not survive the century.`,statements:[{text:`Bleaching always kills corals immediately.`,answer:!1},{text:`The algae live inside the coral and supply it with food.`,answer:!0},{text:`Major bleaching events are happening more often than before.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most researchers believe that breeding heat-tolerant corals will solve the problem on its own.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-022`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the software is 'freely available'; analysts fear people may doubt 'genuine evidence too'; detection is an 'arms race'; and origin-tracking schemes raise privacy concerns.`,passage:`Not long ago, convincingly faking a video of a public figure required a professional studio and considerable expertise. Today, software capable of producing such forgeries is freely available, and the results are improving at a startling pace. The obvious danger is deception: a fabricated clip of a politician making inflammatory remarks could spread around the world before anyone had time to expose it. Yet some analysts argue that an equally serious threat is more subtle. As people grow aware that any recording might be fake, they may begin to doubt genuine evidence too, allowing those caught on camera to dismiss authentic footage as a forgery. Researchers have developed tools to detect manipulated media, but they are locked in an arms race with the forgers, each advance on one side prompting a countermeasure on the other. Some technology companies are now promoting systems that attach a verifiable record of origin to photographs and videos at the moment they are captured. Such schemes could help, but only if they are widely adopted, and critics note that they may raise concerns of their own about privacy and surveillance.`,statements:[{text:`Software for producing fake videos is now easy to obtain.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some analysts fear that real recordings may be dismissed as fake.`,answer:!0},{text:`Detection tools have now permanently defeated the forgers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Critics say that systems recording the origin of images raise no concerns.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-023`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says isolation carries risks 'comparable to' smoking; the reasons 'are not fully understood'; loneliness 'is not the same as being alone'; and whether initiatives work 'remains an open question'.`,passage:`Loneliness has traditionally been treated as a private sorrow rather than a matter for doctors. A growing body of research, however, suggests that persistent social isolation carries health risks comparable to well-established dangers such as smoking and physical inactivity. People who report feeling chronically lonely are more likely to suffer from heart disease, depression and cognitive decline, and some large studies have linked isolation to a higher risk of early death. The reasons are not fully understood. Lonely individuals may be less likely to exercise, eat well or seek medical help, but there is also evidence that isolation itself triggers stress responses that, over time, damage the body. Importantly, loneliness is not the same as being alone: some people live by themselves quite contentedly, while others feel isolated in the midst of family and friends. This distinction complicates efforts to tackle the problem. Several governments have appointed officials responsible for addressing loneliness, and some doctors now prescribe community activities, from gardening groups to choirs, alongside conventional treatment. Whether such initiatives make a lasting difference remains, for now, an open question.`,statements:[{text:`Research suggests loneliness may be as harmful as some well-known health risks.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists fully understand why loneliness damages health.`,answer:!1},{text:`Anyone who lives alone is bound to feel lonely.`,answer:!1},{text:`It is not yet clear whether official initiatives against loneliness are effective.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-024`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says Earth's rotation is not 'perfectly regular'; leap seconds have been inserted 'since 1972'; they have caused crashes; and astronomers 'were among the few to object'.`,passage:`Atomic clocks are so precise that the best of them would lose less than a second over the entire age of the universe. Their accuracy, paradoxically, has created a problem. Our traditional sense of time is based on the rotation of the Earth, which is neither perfectly regular nor constant; tides, earthquakes and movements deep within the planet cause it to speed up and slow down slightly. To keep atomic time in step with the Earth, timekeepers have, since 1972, occasionally inserted an extra second, known as a leap second, into the world's clocks. For most people, this adjustment passes unnoticed. For computer systems, however, it has proved surprisingly troublesome. Software that assumes every minute contains exactly sixty seconds can misbehave when faced with sixty-one, and several leap seconds have been followed by crashes affecting websites and airline booking systems. Some technology companies resorted to smearing the extra second across an entire day. Weary of such complications, an international meeting in 2022 voted to abandon leap seconds by 2035, accepting that atomic time and solar time will slowly drift apart. Astronomers, who depend on the link, were among the few to object.`,statements:[{text:`The Earth rotates at a perfectly constant speed.`,answer:!1},{text:`Leap seconds have been added to clocks since the early 1970s.`,answer:!0},{text:`Leap seconds have caused problems for some computer systems.`,answer:!0},{text:`Astronomers strongly supported the decision to abandon leap seconds.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-025`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says birds use 'several sources' of information; young birds appear to learn star patterns; the eye hypothesis 'remains difficult to test'; and artificial light can disrupt navigation.`,passage:`Every autumn, songbirds weighing no more than a few grams set off on journeys of thousands of kilometres, often travelling at night and frequently returning to the very same garden the following spring. How they find their way has fascinated scientists for centuries. It is now clear that birds draw on several sources of information at once. They can orient themselves using the position of the sun and, at night, the pattern of stars around the celestial pole, which young birds appear to learn by watching the sky rotate. Smell and familiar landmarks also play a role, particularly towards the end of a journey. Most intriguing is their ability to sense the Earth's magnetic field. One leading hypothesis proposes that light-sensitive molecules in the eye undergo chemical reactions influenced by magnetism, so that birds may literally see the field as a pattern overlaid on their vision. The idea remains difficult to test directly, and some researchers suspect that tiny magnetic particles in the beak are involved as well. What is undeniable is that artificial light and electromagnetic noise from human activity can disrupt these delicate systems.`,statements:[{text:`Birds rely on a single method of navigation.`,answer:!1},{text:`Young birds seem to learn the night sky by observing it.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists have conclusively proved that birds see the magnetic field.`,answer:!1},{text:`Human activity can interfere with birds' ability to navigate.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-026`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says two-digit years saved expensive memory; some people stockpiled food; only 'scattered minor glitches' occurred; and engineers credit years of work for the lack of disaster.`,passage:`As the year 2000 approached, governments and businesses spent hundreds of billions of dollars preparing for what became known as the millennium bug. Many older computer programs stored years using only two digits, a shortcut adopted when memory was expensive, and there were fears that on the first day of the new millennium they would interpret 00 as 1900, causing failures in everything from banking to power supplies. Doom-laden predictions filled newspapers, and some people stockpiled food and withdrew cash. When midnight passed with only scattered minor glitches, a widespread conclusion was that the whole affair had been overblown, perhaps even a lucrative scam by consultants. Many of the engineers involved see it very differently. In their view, the absence of disaster was precisely the result of years of tedious work, during which millions of lines of code were checked and corrected. This is a recurring frustration for those who work in prevention: when they succeed, nothing visible happens, and their success can easily be mistaken for proof that there was never a problem in the first place. The episode is still cited in debates about how to prepare for other risks.`,statements:[{text:`Programmers used two digits for years partly to save memory.`,answer:!0},{text:`Nobody took the predictions of disaster seriously.`,answer:!1},{text:`Major failures occurred across banking systems at midnight.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many engineers believe the preparation work prevented serious problems.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-027`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says risks remain 'even if they exercise regularly'; prolonged standing causes back pain and swollen veins; 'variety' matters most; and many users end up sitting most of the day.`,passage:`The discovery that people who sit for long periods face higher risks of heart disease and diabetes, even if they exercise regularly, prompted a minor revolution in office furniture. Standing desks, once a curiosity, became a fixture of fashionable workplaces, and some enthusiasts declared that sitting was the new smoking. The reality, as so often, is more nuanced. Standing still for hours carries problems of its own, including back pain and swollen veins, which is why jobs that involve prolonged standing are hardly known for their health benefits. What seems to matter most is not posture as such but variety: breaking up long stretches of inactivity with movement, however brief. Studies suggest that getting up every half hour to walk around, climb a flight of stairs or simply stretch can improve blood sugar control and circulation. Standing desks can help if they encourage people to shift position frequently, but many users, after an initial burst of enthusiasm, end up sitting at them most of the day anyway. The most effective intervention, researchers suggest, may be the least glamorous one: building regular movement into the routine of the working day.`,statements:[{text:`Regular exercise completely cancels out the risks of sitting for long periods.`,answer:!1},{text:`Standing for long periods can also cause health problems.`,answer:!0},{text:`The text suggests that frequent short breaks for movement are beneficial.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most people who buy standing desks use them for standing all day.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-028`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the name is 'somewhat misleading'; the cold was not uniform; the Thames froze often enough for fairs; and critics caution against blaming climate alone.`,passage:`Between roughly the fourteenth and nineteenth centuries, much of the Northern Hemisphere experienced a period of cooler temperatures that historians have come to call the Little Ice Age. The name is somewhat misleading. It was not a true ice age, nor was the cold uniform; some decades were mild, and the timing of the coldest spells varied considerably from region to region. Nonetheless, its effects were real and at times devastating. Alpine glaciers advanced, swallowing farms and villages, and the River Thames in London froze solid often enough for crowds to hold fairs on the ice. More seriously, shorter growing seasons and failed harvests led to famine, disease and social unrest across Europe. Scientists continue to debate the causes, which probably included a series of large volcanic eruptions and a period of reduced solar activity. Some historians have gone further, linking the cooling to wars, witch hunts and political upheavals, although critics caution against attributing complex human events to climate alone. The episode remains of particular interest today, when it offers a vivid illustration of how even modest shifts in temperature can disrupt fragile societies.`,statements:[{text:`The Little Ice Age was a true ice age.`,answer:!1},{text:`Temperatures were cold everywhere at the same time throughout the period.`,answer:!1},{text:`Fairs were sometimes held on the frozen River Thames.`,answer:!0},{text:`All historians agree that the cooling caused wars and witch hunts.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-029`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says portrait painters lost clients; artists explored what photography 'could not easily capture'; many painters 'secretly used photographs'; and early photographers imitated classical compositions.`,passage:`When photography was introduced in the late 1830s, some painters feared that their profession was doomed. A machine could now record a face or a landscape with a precision no human hand could match, and portrait painters in particular soon found their clients drifting towards the new studios. One celebrated painter is said to have declared that from that day on, painting was dead. Nothing of the sort happened, of course, but the arrival of the camera did profoundly change the course of art. Freed from the obligation to imitate the visible world faithfully, many artists began to explore what photography could not easily capture: the play of light and colour, the sensation of movement, the inner world of feeling. Some historians see this as one of the forces behind the rise of Impressionism and, eventually, abstraction. The relationship was not purely one of rivalry. Many painters secretly used photographs as aids, and early photographers often imitated the compositions of classical painting in an effort to have their work accepted as art. Over time, each medium came to define itself partly in contrast to the other.`,statements:[{text:`Portrait painters lost some business to photographic studios.`,answer:!0},{text:`Photography caused painting to disappear as a profession.`,answer:!1},{text:`No painters ever used photographs to help them work.`,answer:!1},{text:`Early photographers sometimes copied the compositions of traditional paintings.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-030`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the causes were 'not, for the most part, outright fraud'; psychology was 'by no means the only field affected'; researchers now publish plans in advance; and trust 'remains to be seen'.`,passage:`In the early 2010s, psychology was shaken by an uncomfortable discovery. When researchers attempted to repeat dozens of well-known experiments, many of the original findings failed to reappear, or appeared in a much weaker form. Effects that had been described in textbooks and popular books, and in some cases had inspired public policies, suddenly looked doubtful. The causes were not, for the most part, outright fraud. Rather, they lay in common research practices: studies with too few participants, a tendency to try several analyses and report only the one that produced a striking result, and journals that favoured surprising positive findings over dull confirmations or failures. Psychology was by no means the only field affected; similar problems have since been identified in medicine, economics and biology. The crisis has prompted considerable reform. Many researchers now publish their plans before collecting data, share their raw results openly and conduct larger studies, while some journals agree to publish papers based on the quality of the design regardless of the outcome. Whether these changes will restore public trust remains to be seen, but many scientists regard the episode as a sign that science's self-correcting mechanisms ultimately work.`,statements:[{text:`Most failed findings were the result of deliberate fraud.`,answer:!1},{text:`Similar problems have been found in other scientific fields.`,answer:!0},{text:`Many researchers now announce their plans before gathering data.`,answer:!0},{text:`Public trust in psychology has already been fully restored.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-031`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says wolves had been absent 'some seventy years'; other factors such as hunting and winters affected elk; vegetation has recovered 'far less' in places; and the reintroduction was not a failure.`,passage:`When wolves were reintroduced to Yellowstone National Park in the mid-1990s, after an absence of some seventy years, the results seemed almost too good to be true. According to a widely shared account, the predators reduced the number of elk and changed their behaviour, allowing willows and aspens to recover along riverbanks. The returning trees, in turn, stabilised the soil, provided food for beavers and even altered the course of rivers. The story became a celebrated example of a trophic cascade, in which effects ripple down through a food chain, and it has been used to promote rewilding projects around the world. More recent studies, though, suggest that the picture is considerably messier. Elk numbers were also affected by hunting by humans, harsh winters and the return of bears and cougars, and in many places the vegetation has recovered far less than the popular version implies. Some ecologists worry that an oversimplified narrative may create unrealistic expectations elsewhere. None of this means the reintroduction was a failure; wolves have clearly reshaped the ecosystem. The lesson is rather that nature rarely obeys tidy stories.`,statements:[{text:`Wolves had been missing from the park for about seventy years.`,answer:!0},{text:`Wolves were the only factor that reduced elk numbers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Vegetation recovered everywhere exactly as the popular account describes.`,answer:!1},{text:`The text concludes that the reintroduction of wolves was a failure.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-032`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says most traffic goes through ocean cables, not satellites; cables in deep water rest on the seabed; most damage is accidental; and users rarely notice because traffic can be rerouted.`,passage:`Most people imagine that international internet traffic travels through satellites. In fact, the overwhelming majority of it flows through cables lying on the ocean floor, some of them no thicker than a garden hose. Several hundred of these cables crisscross the planet, carrying everything from financial transactions to video calls at the speed of light. Laying them is a remarkable feat of engineering: specialised ships unspool thousands of kilometres of cable, which in deeper waters simply rests on the seabed, while near coasts it is buried to protect it from anchors and fishing gear. Damage is surprisingly common, with dozens of faults occurring every year, most of them caused by accident rather than sabotage. Repairs require a ship to locate the broken section, haul it to the surface and splice it together, a process that can take days or even weeks. Because traffic can usually be rerouted through other cables, users rarely notice. Nevertheless, security analysts have grown increasingly concerned about the vulnerability of this infrastructure, especially as many cables converge at a small number of landing points, and as ownership has shifted from telecommunications firms towards large technology companies.`,statements:[{text:`Satellites carry most international internet traffic.`,answer:!1},{text:`In deep water, the cables are usually buried beneath the seabed.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most damage to the cables is accidental.`,answer:!0},{text:`Users rarely notice when a cable is damaged.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-033`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says deaths were higher among women attended by doctors; handwashing made the death rate fall 'dramatically'; his manner and reluctance to publish hurt his case; and acceptance came after germ theory.`,passage:`In the 1840s, a young doctor working in a Vienna maternity hospital noticed something disturbing. Women whose babies were delivered by doctors and medical students died of fever far more often than those attended by midwives. After a colleague died from an infection contracted during an autopsy, he concluded that doctors were carrying invisible particles from the dissecting room to the delivery ward. When he insisted that staff wash their hands in a chlorine solution, the death rate fell dramatically. One might expect such a result to have been embraced at once. Instead, his ideas met fierce resistance. Many physicians were offended by the suggestion that gentlemen like themselves could be the cause of their patients' deaths, and the doctor's own abrasive manner and reluctance to publish his findings did little to win them over. He lost his position and died in an asylum some years later, ironically from an infected wound. Only after the germ theory of disease was established did his insight gain general acceptance. Today he is honoured as a pioneer, and his name is attached to the reflex rejection of new evidence that contradicts established beliefs.`,statements:[{text:`Women attended by midwives died more often than those attended by doctors.`,answer:!1},{text:`Handwashing led to a sharp fall in deaths.`,answer:!0},{text:`The doctor published his findings widely and promptly.`,answer:!1},{text:`His ideas were generally accepted only after germ theory was established.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-034`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the term was coined in the nineteenth century; few merchants travelled the whole distance; papermaking spread westwards; and diseases followed the trade.`,passage:`The phrase Silk Road conjures up an image of a single highway along which caravans laden with silk plodded steadily from China to Rome. Historians increasingly regard this picture as misleading. The term itself was coined only in the nineteenth century by a German geographer, and the routes it describes were in reality a shifting web of paths across deserts, mountains and seas, connecting countless towns and markets. Few merchants travelled the entire distance; goods typically passed through many hands, each trader carrying them a short way before selling them on. Silk was only one commodity among many, alongside spices, horses, paper, glass and precious metals. Perhaps more significant than any of these goods were the ideas and technologies that moved along the same routes. Religions such as Buddhism spread eastwards, while techniques for making paper travelled westwards, eventually transforming European learning. Diseases, too, followed the trade, with devastating consequences. Some scholars now prefer to speak of the Silk Roads, in the plural, to capture this complexity. Whatever the label, the network illustrates how deeply connected the medieval world was, long before modern globalisation.`,statements:[{text:`The term Silk Road was used by ancient merchants themselves.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most merchants carried their goods all the way from China to Rome.`,answer:!1},{text:`Knowledge of papermaking spread from east to west along these routes.`,answer:!0},{text:`The trade routes also helped spread disease.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-035`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says forgers have fooled experts; forgeries 'distort our understanding' of artists; one anachronistic material can expose a fake; and science 'cannot settle every case'.`,passage:`Art forgers occupy a curious place in the public imagination. Rather than being regarded simply as criminals, the most notorious of them are often portrayed as charming rogues who exposed the snobbery and gullibility of the art world. There is some justice in this view. Forgers have repeatedly fooled eminent experts, museums and wealthy collectors, sometimes with works that later seem embarrassingly crude. In several cases, the experts' desire to discover a lost masterpiece appears to have clouded their judgement. Yet the romantic image obscures real harm. Forgeries distort our understanding of an artist's development, and once accepted into catalogues and collections they can be extraordinarily difficult to remove. Buyers lose fortunes, and genuine works may come under suspicion. Modern techniques, from chemical analysis of pigments to the dating of canvas fibres, have made forgery considerably riskier, since a single anachronistic material can betray an otherwise flawless imitation. Even so, scientific methods cannot settle every case, and attribution still depends heavily on the trained eye of the connoisseur. Some specialists estimate that a significant proportion of works on the market may not be what they claim to be.`,statements:[{text:`Forgers have sometimes deceived respected experts.`,answer:!0},{text:`Forgeries do no real harm to the study of art.`,answer:!1},{text:`A single material from the wrong period can reveal a forgery.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientific analysis can now resolve every question of attribution.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-036`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says researchers first suspected faulty instruments; industry 'initially resisted'; full recovery will take 'several decades'; and ozone alternatives were 'relatively cheap'.`,passage:`In the 1970s and 1980s, scientists made an alarming discovery: a thinning of the ozone layer, the region of the upper atmosphere that shields life on Earth from harmful ultraviolet radiation. Over Antarctica, the losses each spring were so severe that researchers initially suspected faulty instruments. The culprits turned out to be chlorofluorocarbons, synthetic chemicals widely used in refrigerators, aerosol sprays and foam packaging, which drift into the stratosphere and release chlorine that destroys ozone. What followed is often hailed as one of the great success stories of international cooperation. In 1987, governments agreed on a treaty to phase out the offending chemicals, and it was later strengthened several times and eventually ratified by every country in the world. Industry, which had initially resisted, developed substitutes more quickly than expected. The ozone layer is now slowly healing, though full recovery is not expected for several decades because the chemicals linger in the atmosphere. Commentators frequently ask why a similar approach has proved so much harder for climate change. Part of the answer is that alternatives to the ozone-destroying chemicals were relatively cheap, whereas fossil fuels are woven into almost every aspect of the economy.`,statements:[{text:`Researchers initially doubted their measurements over Antarctica.`,answer:!0},{text:`Industry supported the restrictions from the very beginning.`,answer:!1},{text:`The ozone layer has already fully recovered.`,answer:!1},{text:`Replacing the ozone-destroying chemicals was relatively inexpensive.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-037`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says octopus intelligence 'evolved entirely independently'; some species carry coconut shells as shelters; about two thirds of neurons are in the arms; and most octopuses live 'only a year or two'.`,passage:`Octopuses have long confounded our assumptions about intelligence. Their last common ancestor with humans was probably a simple worm-like creature that lived more than half a billion years ago, so whatever cleverness they possess evolved entirely independently of our own. And clever they certainly appear to be. In laboratories, octopuses have learned to open screw-top jars, navigate mazes and recognise individual human keepers, sometimes squirting water at those they seem to dislike. In the wild, some species collect coconut shells and carry them around for use as portable shelters. Their nervous system is organised in a way quite unlike ours: around two thirds of their neurons are located not in the brain but in the arms, each of which appears capable of a degree of independent action. This has led some researchers to wonder what it might be like, if it is like anything at all, to be such an animal. Curiously, most octopuses live for only a year or two and receive no care from their parents, which raises the question of why such complex abilities evolved in creatures with so little time to use them.`,statements:[{text:`Octopus intelligence evolved from a recent ancestor shared with humans.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some wild octopuses use coconut shells as shelters.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most of an octopus's neurons are located in its brain.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most octopuses have a short lifespan.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-038`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says farmers have been 'among the most vocal supporters'; manufacturers cite safety and intellectual property; several jurisdictions have passed laws; and critics suspect some changes are only public relations.`,passage:`Anyone who has tried to fix a broken smartphone or replace the battery in a laptop will be familiar with the obstacles: components glued in place, unusual screws that require special tools, and manufacturers that refuse to supply spare parts or repair manuals to independent shops. Campaigners for the so-called right to repair argue that such practices push consumers to replace devices that could easily have been mended, adding to mountains of electronic waste and draining their wallets. Farmers have been among the most vocal supporters, complaining that modern tractors contain software locks that prevent them from carrying out even simple repairs without an authorised technician. Manufacturers counter that unrestricted access could compromise safety, security and intellectual property, and that amateur repairs may damage products. In recent years, however, the campaign has gained considerable ground. Several jurisdictions, including the European Union and a number of American states, have passed laws obliging companies to make parts, tools and information available for a period of years. Some firms have responded by redesigning products to be more easily repaired, though critics suspect that, in certain cases, the changes amount to little more than public relations.`,statements:[{text:`Farmers have strongly supported the right-to-repair campaign.`,answer:!0},{text:`Manufacturers argue that unrestricted repair access could create safety risks.`,answer:!0},{text:`No government has yet passed right-to-repair legislation.`,answer:!1},{text:`Critics are convinced that every product redesign is sincere.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-039`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the change 'cannot be explained by genetics alone'; the evidence for close work is 'weaker than many assume'; outdoor time protects children 'regardless of how much they read'; and daylight is brighter than indoor light 'even on a cloudy day'.`,passage:`Rates of short-sightedness have risen so sharply in recent decades that some researchers speak of an epidemic. In parts of East Asia, the large majority of young adults now need glasses to see distant objects clearly, compared with a small minority a few generations ago. Such a rapid change cannot be explained by genetics alone, and attention has naturally turned to the environment. For years, the main suspect was close work such as reading and, more recently, staring at screens. The evidence for this link exists but is weaker than many assume. A more consistent finding concerns time spent outdoors. Children who spend more hours outside are considerably less likely to become short-sighted, regardless of how much they read, and the protective factor seems to be the intensity of natural daylight, which is far brighter than indoor lighting even on a cloudy day. Several trials in schools that added outdoor time to the timetable reported fewer new cases. Researchers have also developed special lenses and eye drops that slow progression in children who are already affected, but the simplest advice for parents remains surprisingly old-fashioned: send them out to play.`,statements:[{text:`Genetic factors alone can explain the rise in short-sightedness.`,answer:!1},{text:`The link between reading and short-sightedness is stronger than most people think.`,answer:!1},{text:`Spending time outdoors seems to protect children's eyesight.`,answer:!0},{text:`Daylight on a cloudy day is dimmer than typical indoor lighting.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-040`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the potato was first met 'with deep suspicion'; it gave more calories per acre than grain; the guarded field is a 'popular legend' that may not be true; and a disease devastated Irish harvests.`,passage:`It is hard to imagine European cooking without the potato, yet when the plant first arrived from South America in the sixteenth century, it was greeted with deep suspicion. As a member of the nightshade family, it was related to several poisonous species, and some people believed it caused leprosy or other diseases. Others objected that it was not mentioned in the Bible. For many decades it was grown mainly as a botanical curiosity or as food for animals. Its eventual triumph owed a good deal to persistent promotion by rulers and reformers who recognised its value. The potato produced far more calories per acre than grain, grew in poor soils and could be left in the ground until needed, making it less vulnerable to plundering armies. According to a popular legend, one monarch had a field of potatoes guarded by soldiers during the day, so that local peasants would conclude the crop must be precious and steal it at night. Whether or not the tale is true, the potato's spread contributed to a remarkable growth in population. Its dangers became tragically apparent, however, when a single disease devastated harvests in nineteenth-century Ireland.`,statements:[{text:`The potato was welcomed enthusiastically as soon as it reached Europe.`,answer:!1},{text:`Potatoes yielded more calories per acre than grain.`,answer:!0},{text:`The story of the guarded potato field is confirmed by historians.`,answer:!1},{text:`Disease destroyed potato harvests in nineteenth-century Ireland.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-041`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the name comes from the French for raw concrete; problems were often due to 'poor maintenance and underfunding'; the style has enjoyed a 'revival'; and well-maintained examples have proved popular with residents.`,passage:`Few architectural styles divide opinion as sharply as brutalism. The name derives not from brutality but from the French term for raw concrete, the material its practitioners used with uncompromising honesty, leaving surfaces unpainted and often bearing the imprint of the wooden moulds in which they were cast. In the decades after the Second World War, brutalist buildings rose across the world as universities, housing estates, libraries and government offices, embodying ambitions of social progress and civic dignity. By the 1980s, many had come to be seen as grim and oppressive, blamed for social problems that had more to do with poor maintenance and underfunding than with design. A number were demolished amid little protest. Recently, however, the style has enjoyed an unexpected revival of affection. Photographs of brutalist structures circulate widely online, books celebrate their sculptural boldness, and campaigns have been launched to save threatened examples. Critics of the revival suspect that some of this enthusiasm is aesthetic nostalgia from people who have never had to live in a damp concrete flat. Supporters reply that the buildings' fate should not depend on passing fashion, and that well-maintained examples have often proved highly popular with their residents.`,statements:[{text:`The name brutalism refers to the cruelty of the buildings.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some of the problems blamed on brutalist design were caused by a lack of maintenance.`,answer:!0},{text:`Interest in brutalism has grown again in recent years.`,answer:!0},{text:`Residents have always disliked brutalist buildings, however well maintained.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-042`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the discoverer 'struggled to purify' penicillin; it remained a curiosity 'for about a decade'; the drug was once recovered from urine; and the Nobel Prize was shared among three people.`,passage:`The discovery of penicillin is usually told as a tale of happy accident. In 1928, a Scottish bacteriologist returned from holiday to find that a mould had contaminated one of his culture plates and that the bacteria around it had died. The story is true as far as it goes, but it omits a great deal. The discoverer published his observations, yet he struggled to purify the substance and soon turned his attention elsewhere, doubting that it could be produced in useful quantities. For about a decade, penicillin remained a laboratory curiosity. It was a team at Oxford, working at the end of the 1930s, that developed methods to extract and concentrate the drug and demonstrated its remarkable power in treating infections. Even then, production was painfully slow; in one early case, the drug was recovered from a patient's urine so that it could be used again. Mass manufacture was achieved only with the help of American industry during the Second World War. The Nobel Prize was eventually shared between the original discoverer and two members of the Oxford team. The episode shows how much painstaking work can lie behind a single moment of insight.`,statements:[{text:`The discoverer of penicillin quickly found a way to produce it in large quantities.`,answer:!1},{text:`For roughly ten years, penicillin was of little practical use.`,answer:!0},{text:`Early supplies were so scarce that the drug was sometimes reused.`,answer:!0},{text:`The Nobel Prize went to the original discoverer alone.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-043`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says locusts are normally solitary; contact between hind legs triggers the change; control is most effective when countries cooperate; and biological pesticides are being tested.`,passage:`For most of its life, the desert locust is a shy, solitary insect that avoids the company of its own kind. When heavy rains produce a sudden abundance of vegetation, however, populations can multiply rapidly, and as the insects are crowded together, a remarkable transformation takes place. Repeated contact between their hind legs triggers changes in behaviour and, over successive generations, in colour and body shape. The once-solitary creatures become gregarious, seeking each other out and eventually forming swarms that may contain billions of individuals. A large swarm can travel well over a hundred kilometres in a day and consume, daily, roughly as much food as several million people. Plagues of this kind have threatened harvests across Africa, the Middle East and South Asia since ancient times. Today, control relies mainly on monitoring breeding areas through satellite data and field surveys, and on spraying young locusts before they can take to the air. This approach works best when countries cooperate across borders, which conflict and political instability often make difficult. Some scientists fear that changing rainfall patterns could make outbreaks more frequent, while researchers are also testing biological pesticides as alternatives to chemicals.`,statements:[{text:`Desert locusts normally live in large groups.`,answer:!1},{text:`Physical contact between the insects helps to trigger their transformation.`,answer:!0},{text:`Control efforts are most effective when countries work together.`,answer:!0},{text:`Chemical sprays are the only method currently being researched.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-044`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says early systems relied on rules written by linguists; fluent output can hide errors; problems are worst for languages with little digital text; and many translators now edit machine output.`,passage:`Machine translation has improved so dramatically in recent years that many people now rely on it without a second thought, to read foreign websites, order meals abroad or exchange messages with colleagues. Early systems, which relied on vast sets of grammatical rules written by linguists, produced stilted and often comical results. Modern programs instead learn statistical patterns from enormous collections of existing translations, and their output is frequently fluent enough to pass for human work. That very fluency, however, can be deceptive. Because the systems are designed to produce plausible sentences, an error may be expressed with complete grammatical confidence, making it harder to detect than the clumsy mistakes of earlier software. Problems are most acute in specialised fields such as law and medicine, where a single mistranslated term can have serious consequences, and for languages with relatively little digital text available. Professional translators have not disappeared; many now work by editing machine output rather than starting from scratch, a change some welcome and others resent as a reduction in both pay and creative satisfaction. Whether machines will ever fully grasp humour, irony and cultural nuance remains a matter of lively debate.`,statements:[{text:`Early translation systems learned patterns from large collections of translations.`,answer:!1},{text:`A fluent translation may contain errors that are hard to notice.`,answer:!0},{text:`Machine translation works equally well for all languages.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some professional translators now mainly correct machine-produced text.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-045`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says caffeine 'masks fatigue rather than eliminating it'; some alertness may reflect relief from withdrawal; observational findings 'cannot prove cause and effect'; and genetic differences affect sensitivity.`,passage:`Caffeine is the most widely consumed psychoactive substance in the world, taken daily by a large majority of adults in the form of coffee, tea, soft drinks or chocolate. It works chiefly by blocking the action of adenosine, a chemical that accumulates in the brain during waking hours and promotes a feeling of drowsiness. By occupying the receptors that adenosine would normally bind to, caffeine temporarily masks fatigue rather than eliminating it, which is why the tiredness can return with a vengeance once its effects wear off. Regular consumers develop a degree of tolerance, and some of the alertness they attribute to their morning cup may simply reflect the relief of mild withdrawal symptoms. For most healthy adults, moderate consumption appears safe, and some large studies have even associated coffee drinking with lower risks of certain diseases, although such observational findings cannot prove cause and effect. Sensitivity varies considerably, partly because of genetic differences in how quickly the liver breaks the substance down. Because caffeine can remain active for many hours, experts advise those who struggle to sleep to avoid it from the early afternoon onwards.`,statements:[{text:`Caffeine removes the underlying cause of tiredness.`,answer:!1},{text:`Part of the boost regular drinkers feel may come from relieving withdrawal.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies have proved that coffee prevents certain diseases.`,answer:!1},{text:`People react differently to caffeine partly because of their genes.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-046`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says survivors could demand higher wages; laws to freeze wages were 'difficult to enforce'; living standards rose in parts of western Europe; and the gains were 'neither universal nor permanent'.`,passage:`The Black Death, which swept across Europe in the middle of the fourteenth century, killed somewhere between a third and a half of the continent's population within a few years. The human suffering was beyond measure, yet historians have long noted that, for many of the survivors, the aftermath brought unexpected opportunities. With so many workers dead, labour became scarce, and peasants and craftsmen found themselves in a position to demand higher wages and better conditions. Landowners, faced with empty fields, often had little choice but to comply. Authorities in several countries tried to freeze wages at their earlier levels and restrict the movement of workers, but such laws proved difficult to enforce and bred deep resentment, contributing in some places to popular revolts. Over the following century, the living standards of ordinary people in parts of western Europe appear to have risen noticeably, with diets including more meat and ale. Some economists have even argued that these changes helped to set western Europe on a different economic path from regions where landowners managed to tighten their grip. Such arguments remain contested, and the gains were neither universal nor permanent.`,statements:[{text:`Labour shortages allowed some workers to demand better pay.`,answer:!0},{text:`Laws freezing wages were easily enforced.`,answer:!1},{text:`Ordinary people in parts of western Europe ate more meat in the following century.`,answer:!0},{text:`The economic gains were shared equally by everyone and lasted forever.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-047`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says silent films 'had all but vanished' within a few years; microphones were hidden in objects; star careers ending happened 'less often than legend claims'; and technical advances restored mobility within a decade.`,passage:`The arrival of synchronised sound in the late 1920s transformed the film industry with astonishing speed. Within a few years, silent pictures had all but vanished from cinemas, and studios had invested vast sums in converting theatres and building soundproof stages. The transition was far from smooth. Early recording equipment was cumbersome, and microphones had to be hidden in flower pots or lamps, forcing actors to stay close to them and stand awkwardly still. Cameras, which made a loud whirring noise, were enclosed in bulky booths, robbing films of the fluid movement that silent directors had perfected. Some stars whose voices did not match their screen image, or who struggled with dialogue, saw their careers decline, although historians suggest this happened less often than legend claims. Musicians who had accompanied silent screenings in cinemas lost their jobs in large numbers. Several directors and critics lamented the change, arguing that silent cinema had been developing into a distinctive visual art which talking pictures reduced to filmed theatre. Within a decade, however, technical advances restored much of the lost mobility, and filmmakers learned to use sound creatively rather than simply recording speech.`,statements:[{text:`Silent films remained common in cinemas for decades after sound arrived.`,answer:!1},{text:`Early microphones were sometimes concealed in objects on set.`,answer:!0},{text:`Historians believe the careers of silent stars ended even more often than the legends suggest.`,answer:!1},{text:`Cinema musicians were largely unaffected by the change.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-048`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says geologists thought the Earth 'immensely old'; the physicist's estimate seemed to leave 'too little room for evolution'; his assumptions were flawed because he knew nothing of radioactivity; and the true age exceeded what geologists had supposed.`,passage:`In the nineteenth century, a fierce dispute arose over the age of the Earth. Geologists, studying the slow processes by which rocks are laid down and worn away, were convinced that the planet must be immensely old, and Darwin's theory of evolution seemed to require vast stretches of time for life to diversify. Ranged against them was one of the most respected physicists of the age, who calculated how long it would have taken the Earth to cool from a molten state to its present temperature. His answer, at most a few tens of millions of years, appeared to leave far too little room for evolution, and his authority was such that many scientists deferred to him. The physics behind his reasoning was impeccable, but his assumptions were not. He knew nothing of radioactivity, discovered only at the end of the century, which supplies a continuous source of heat deep within the Earth. Radioactive decay also provided geologists with a means of dating rocks precisely, and by the mid-twentieth century the age of the Earth had been established at around four and a half billion years, far older than even the geologists had dared to suppose.`,statements:[{text:`The physicist's estimate supported the geologists' view of a very old Earth.`,answer:!1},{text:`The physicist's error lay in his assumptions rather than in his physics.`,answer:!0},{text:`Radioactivity was already well understood when he made his calculation.`,answer:!1},{text:`The Earth turned out to be older than even the geologists had believed.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-049`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says greys were introduced in the nineteenth century; they 'do not generally attack' reds; the virus they carry is usually fatal to reds; and reds have begun to reappear where martens returned.`,passage:`The grey squirrel, introduced to Britain from North America in the nineteenth century as a fashionable addition to country estates, has since become one of the country's most successful mammals. Its spread has come at the expense of the native red squirrel, which has disappeared from most of England and Wales and now survives mainly in Scotland and a few isolated refuges. Contrary to popular belief, greys do not generally attack their red cousins. Instead, they outcompete them, being larger, better able to digest certain seeds and more adaptable to varied habitats. Even more damaging is a virus that greys carry without apparent harm but which is usually fatal to reds. Conservationists have tried several approaches to halt the decline, including culling greys in key areas, with mixed results. A promising development has come from an unexpected quarter: the recovery of the pine marten, a predator that seems to target greys more successfully than reds, perhaps because the native squirrels evolved alongside it and are more wary. In regions where martens have returned, grey numbers have fallen and reds have begun to reappear. Researchers caution, however, that the relationship needs further study.`,statements:[{text:`Grey squirrels were brought to Britain from North America.`,answer:!0},{text:`Grey squirrels usually kill red squirrels in fights.`,answer:!1},{text:`The virus carried by grey squirrels also kills most grey squirrels.`,answer:!1},{text:`Red squirrels have started to return in some areas where pine martens have recovered.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-050`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says receivers use signals from 'several satellites'; grids and markets rely on the timing signals; the signals are 'extremely weak' on the ground; and experts urge backup systems.`,passage:`Satellite navigation has become so embedded in daily life that many people can no longer imagine finding their way without it. The system relies on a constellation of satellites, each broadcasting precise time signals from atomic clocks on board; a receiver calculates its position by measuring how long the signals take to arrive from several satellites at once. Less widely appreciated is how much else depends on the same signals. Mobile phone networks, electricity grids and financial markets use them to synchronise their operations, often down to millionths of a second. Because the signals are extremely weak by the time they reach the ground, they are vulnerable to interference, both accidental and deliberate. Cheap jamming devices can block reception over a wide area, and more sophisticated equipment can broadcast false signals, misleading receivers about where they are. Incidents affecting ships and aircraft have become increasingly common in certain regions. Experts have urged governments to invest in backup systems, such as ground-based radio beacons, and to ensure that critical infrastructure can continue functioning if satellite signals fail. Some psychologists add a further concern: that habitual reliance on navigation apps may weaken our own sense of direction.`,statements:[{text:`A receiver can determine its position using a single satellite.`,answer:!1},{text:`Financial markets use satellite signals to coordinate timing.`,answer:!0},{text:`The signals are so strong that they are almost impossible to disrupt.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experts have recommended backup systems in case satellite signals fail.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-051`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says speed was the only way to limit agony; some doctors thought pain was a 'necessary stimulus'; opposition faded after Queen Victoria used chloroform; and anaesthesia alone did not make surgery much safer.`,passage:`Before the middle of the nineteenth century, surgery was a terrifying ordeal. Patients were held down by assistants while surgeons worked as quickly as possible, since speed was the only means of limiting the agony. Alcohol and opium offered some relief but were unreliable and dangerous in large doses. The first public demonstration of ether as an anaesthetic, in Boston in 1846, therefore caused a sensation. Within months, the technique had spread across Europe, and chloroform was soon introduced as an alternative. Remarkably, the use of anaesthesia met some opposition. A few religious commentators argued that pain in childbirth was divinely ordained, and some doctors believed that pain was a necessary stimulus that helped patients survive the shock of an operation. Resistance faded considerably after Queen Victoria accepted chloroform during the birth of one of her children. Anaesthesia did not immediately make surgery much safer, however. Freed from the need for haste, surgeons undertook longer and more ambitious operations, but without an understanding of infection, many patients still died afterwards. Only with the later introduction of antiseptic methods did the combination of painless and safe surgery become a reality.`,statements:[{text:`Before anaesthesia, surgeons operated quickly to reduce suffering.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone welcomed anaesthesia without reservation.`,answer:!1},{text:`Queen Victoria's use of chloroform helped reduce opposition to it.`,answer:!0},{text:`Anaesthesia immediately made surgery much safer.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-052`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says research paints 'a more modest picture'; dramatic stories came from moralising pamphlets; there is 'little evidence of widespread bankruptcies'; and the striking patterns were caused by a virus.`,passage:`In the 1630s, the Dutch Republic experienced what is often described as the first great speculative bubble in history. Tulips, which had only recently arrived from the Ottoman Empire, became objects of intense desire, especially rare varieties whose petals displayed striking flames of colour. Prices rose to extraordinary heights before collapsing abruptly in early 1637. In popular accounts, the episode is told as a tale of collective madness, in which ordinary citizens sold their houses for a single bulb and the national economy was ruined when the market crashed. Recent research based on surviving contracts and records paints a considerably more modest picture. The trade involved a relatively small circle of wealthy merchants and enthusiasts, and many of the most dramatic stories appear to have originated in moralising pamphlets published after the crash, which condemned greed and frivolity. There is little evidence of widespread bankruptcies, and the Dutch economy continued to flourish in the years that followed. Ironically, the colourful patterns that made the rarest tulips so valuable were later found to be caused by a virus, which also weakened the bulbs and made them difficult to propagate.`,statements:[{text:`Recent research supports the view that the whole nation was ruined by the crash.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many dramatic stories about the episode came from pamphlets criticising greed.`,answer:!0},{text:`The Dutch economy continued to prosper after the collapse.`,answer:!0},{text:`The striking patterns on rare tulips were produced by careful breeding alone.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-053`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some claimed women lacked stamina; carpets hid footsteps; later commentators questioned the study's evidence; and other factors also raised women's numbers.`,passage:`For much of the twentieth century, the leading orchestras of Europe and America were overwhelmingly male. Conductors and administrators insisted that they simply chose the best players available, and some even claimed that women lacked the stamina or temperament required for orchestral life. From the 1970s onwards, many orchestras began to hold auditions in which candidates performed behind a screen, so that the panel could hear them but not see them. Some institutions went further, laying carpet on the floor so that the sound of footsteps would not reveal whether the musician was wearing high heels. In the following decades, the proportion of women in these orchestras rose substantially. A widely cited study attributed part of this increase to the screens, although later commentators have questioned how strong its statistical evidence really was, and other factors, such as the growing number of women graduating from music schools, clearly played a role too. Whatever the precise figures, the practice has become a well-known example of how removing irrelevant information can make judgements fairer. Similar ideas have since been applied to recruitment in other fields, for instance by removing names from job applications.`,statements:[{text:`Some people once argued that women were unsuited to orchestral work.`,answer:!0},{text:`Carpets were sometimes used so that the panel could not hear candidates' footsteps.`,answer:!0},{text:`Everyone accepts that the famous study's evidence is beyond question.`,answer:!1},{text:`Screens were the only reason more women joined orchestras.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-054`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the album helped the campaign against whaling; males in a population sing 'broadly the same song'; new songs spread between populations; and the purpose of the singing 'remains uncertain'.`,passage:`In the late 1960s, recordings of humpback whales became an unlikely bestseller. Listeners were captivated by the long, haunting sequences of moans, cries and whistles, and the album helped to fuel the campaign that eventually led to a moratorium on commercial whaling. Scientists, meanwhile, were discovering that these sounds were far more structured than anyone had suspected. Male humpbacks produce songs made up of repeated themes, each composed of shorter phrases, which may last for half an hour and be repeated for hours on end. All the males in a given population sing broadly the same song, yet the song gradually changes over the course of a breeding season, with every singer adopting the changes. Even more striking, entirely new songs occasionally sweep across ocean basins, spreading from one population to another within a couple of years, a phenomenon some researchers have compared to the spread of a hit song among humans. The purpose of the singing remains uncertain. It is most often heard during the breeding season, which suggests a role in courtship or competition, but females rarely seem to approach singing males, and the full explanation continues to elude researchers.`,statements:[{text:`The popularity of whale recordings helped the campaign against commercial whaling.`,answer:!0},{text:`Each male in a population sings a completely different song.`,answer:!1},{text:`New songs can spread from one whale population to another.`,answer:!0},{text:`Scientists have fully explained why humpback whales sing.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-055`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says spending on cars and holidays 'collapses', the pattern appears 'in one recession but not in the next', sceptics blame 'selective storytelling', and the products 'vary from one period to another'.`,passage:`Economists have long been puzzled by what some call the 'lipstick effect': the observation that, during downturns, sales of small luxuries often hold steady or even rise while spending on cars, holidays and furniture collapses. The usual explanation is that consumers who can no longer afford large indulgences redirect a fraction of that money towards modest treats, which offer a sense of normality at a price they can justify. Tempting as this account is, the evidence for it is patchier than its popularity suggests. Several studies have found the pattern in one recession but not in the next, and the products involved vary from one period to another: in some decades it was cosmetics, in others chocolate, cinema tickets or nail varnish. Sceptics argue that the effect owes more to selective storytelling than to any stable law of behaviour, since journalists naturally seize on the categories that happen to buck the trend. Others counter that the underlying mechanism may be real even if the specific goods change, because what matters is not the item itself but its role as an affordable reward. Settling the dispute would require consistent data across many countries, which has so far proved elusive.`,statements:[{text:`The products that benefit from the effect have stayed the same over time.`,answer:!1},{text:`Researchers have found the lipstick effect consistently in every recession studied.`,answer:!1},{text:`During recessions, spending on large purchases such as cars tends to fall sharply.`,answer:!0},{text:`Sceptics suspect that media reporting may exaggerate the effect.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-056`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the decision was driven 'by arithmetic' (fuel bills and vacancies), parents 'were divided', maths scores 'have dipped', and the clubs are 'staffed largely by volunteers'.`,passage:`When a rural school district in the north of the country introduced a four-day week, the decision was driven less by educational theory than by arithmetic. Faced with rising fuel bills and a shrinking pool of applicants for teaching posts, administrators reasoned that closing on Fridays would cut transport costs and make vacancies more attractive. Parents were divided from the outset. Families with flexible working hours welcomed the long weekend, whereas those in shift work found themselves scrambling for childcare. Three years on, the results are ambiguous. Teacher recruitment has undeniably improved, and staff report lower levels of exhaustion. Attendance, too, has risen slightly, perhaps because medical appointments can now be scheduled on the day off. Yet test scores in mathematics have dipped among younger pupils, and researchers caution that the extra length of the remaining four days may tax the concentration of seven- and eight-year-olds. Some schools have responded by offering optional Friday clubs, staffed largely by volunteers, though uptake has been uneven. Whether the model can be sustained without quietly shifting its costs onto families remains, as one local councillor put it, the question nobody wants to answer.`,statements:[{text:`The four-day week was introduced mainly for financial and staffing reasons.`,answer:!0},{text:`All parents supported the change when it was first announced.`,answer:!1},{text:`Mathematics results among younger pupils have improved since the change.`,answer:!1},{text:`The Friday clubs depend mostly on unpaid helpers.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-057`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text mentions cultures 'over a century old', says a moved starter 'takes on the microbial character of its new home', states the finding 'need not diminish the romance', and says buyers 'may be paying chiefly for a story'.`,passage:`Few culinary traditions are as fiercely guarded as those surrounding sourdough. Bakers who maintain a starter, the bubbling mixture of flour and water that provides the bread's leavening, often speak of it with the tenderness usually reserved for pets, and some boast of cultures said to be over a century old. Microbiologists, however, have injected a note of scepticism into these claims. When researchers sampled starters from bakeries on several continents, they found that the community of yeasts and bacteria inside each jar was shaped far more by the baker's own hands and kitchen than by the culture's supposed ancestry. A starter shipped from one city to another, in other words, gradually takes on the microbial character of its new home. This finding need not diminish the romance of the craft. If anything, it suggests that every loaf carries a faint signature of the person who made it, a notion many bakers have found rather appealing. What it does undermine is the commercial practice of selling 'heritage' starters at premium prices on the promise that they will reproduce the flavour of a distant region. Buyers may be paying chiefly for a story.`,statements:[{text:`Some bakers claim their starters are more than a hundred years old.`,answer:!0},{text:`The research showed that a starter keeps its original microbes wherever it is taken.`,answer:!1},{text:`The author believes the findings completely destroy the appeal of sourdough baking.`,answer:!1},{text:`The text casts doubt on the value of expensive 'heritage' starters.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-058`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says oral agreements 'can be enforceable', writing serves 'chiefly as evidence', land transactions 'generally require a written document', and courts treat emojis as 'possible indications of assent'.`,passage:`The notion that a contract must be signed in ink to be binding is one of the more persistent misconceptions in everyday legal thinking. In most common-law jurisdictions, an agreement can be enforceable even if it was concluded orally, provided that there was an offer, an acceptance, an intention to create legal relations and something of value exchanged by each side. Writing serves chiefly as evidence: it is far easier to prove what was agreed when the terms are set down on paper or in an email. There are, admittedly, important exceptions. Transactions involving land, for instance, generally require a written document, as do certain guarantees, and legislators have imposed formal requirements on consumer credit to protect borrowers from hasty commitments. Courts have also grown accustomed to treating clicks, typed names and even emojis as possible indications of assent, provided that the context makes the party's intention clear. The difficulty, then, lies less in forming a contract than in proving one. Lawyers routinely advise clients that a handshake may well be legally sufficient, but that relying on it is an invitation to an expensive dispute about who promised what.`,statements:[{text:`According to the text, the main function of writing is to make a contract valid.`,answer:!1},{text:`Agreements about land are usually an exception to the general rule.`,answer:!0},{text:`Courts always refuse to treat an emoji as a sign of agreement.`,answer:!1},{text:`In most common-law systems, a spoken agreement can be legally binding.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-059`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says places are 'allocated by ballot', the typical runner is 'neither young nor especially athletic', many start 'after a personal upheaval', and critics call the sport 'a marker of affluence'.`,passage:`Marathon running was once regarded as an eccentric pursuit, the preserve of a few lean obsessives willing to endure hours of discomfort for no obvious reward. Today, big-city races attract tens of thousands of entrants, and places in the most famous events are allocated by ballot because demand so vastly outstrips supply. Sociologists who study the phenomenon point out that the typical participant is neither young nor especially athletic. Many are in their forties, take up the sport after a personal upheaval such as a divorce or bereavement, and describe the training as a way of imposing order on lives that feel otherwise chaotic. The race itself, in this reading, functions as a rite of passage: a public, measurable trial whose completion confers a sense of renewal. Critics are less charitable. They note that entry fees, travel and equipment can easily run into thousands, turning what was supposed to be the most democratic of sports into a marker of affluence. Charity fundraising, they add, sometimes lends a veneer of altruism to what is essentially a personal project. Supporters reply that it hardly matters why people run, so long as the money raised reaches those who need it.`,statements:[{text:`Entry to some well-known marathons is decided by drawing lots.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most marathon participants are young people with a strong athletic background.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some people take up marathon running after a difficult event in their lives.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics argue that marathons have become cheaper and more accessible than ever.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-060`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the approach removes lights, kerbs and markings, serious collisions 'declined', opposition came 'most notably' from groups for blind people, and some schemes 'reintroduced textured paving'.`,passage:`Planners have spent decades trying to coax people out of their cars, with mixed success. One of the more unexpected victories has come from a deceptively simple measure: removing traffic lights, kerbs and road markings altogether. Known as 'shared space', the approach rests on the idea that drivers who are unsure of their right of way will slow down and negotiate with pedestrians by eye contact rather than relying on signals. In several towns where it has been tried, average speeds have fallen and the number of serious collisions has declined. Yet the concept has attracted vocal opposition, most notably from organisations representing blind and partially sighted people. For someone who cannot see approaching vehicles, the disappearance of a kerb removes the very boundary that once marked where safety ended and danger began, and the reliance on eye contact excludes them by definition. Some schemes have since reintroduced textured paving or low, detectable edges as a compromise. The episode illustrates a wider dilemma in urban design: an intervention that makes a street feel more pleasant and humane for the majority may simultaneously render it hostile to those whose needs are less visible.`,statements:[{text:`Where shared space has been tried, serious accidents have generally become more frequent.`,answer:!1},{text:`Shared space schemes remove signs and markings that normally separate traffic from pedestrians.`,answer:!0},{text:`No changes have been made to any of the schemes since they were introduced.`,answer:!1},{text:`Groups representing blind people have been among the strongest critics of the idea.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-061`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text contrasts the ladder with a climbing wall, says 'old prejudices die hard', states women 'are disproportionately penalised', and says the schemes 'remain small' and are concentrated in a few sectors.`,passage:`For much of the last century, the career ladder was the dominant metaphor for working life: employees joined an organisation young, climbed steadily through its ranks and retired with a pension and a carriage clock. Human-resources specialists now prefer the image of a climbing wall, on which people move sideways, pause, and occasionally step down in order to reach a better position later on. The shift reflects real changes in the labour market. Fewer workers expect to stay with a single employer, and many deliberately accept a lateral move to acquire skills that their current role does not offer. Nevertheless, recruiters admit that old prejudices die hard. A gap on a CV, particularly one taken to care for children or elderly relatives, is still viewed with suspicion by some hiring managers, who may interpret it as a lack of commitment. Women, who undertake the bulk of unpaid care, are disproportionately penalised as a result. A number of firms have launched 'returnship' programmes, offering paid placements to people re-entering the workforce after a long break. Early evaluations are encouraging, though the schemes remain small and are concentrated in a handful of well-funded sectors.`,statements:[{text:`Returnship programmes are now widespread across almost every industry.`,answer:!1},{text:`Women are more likely than men to be disadvantaged by career breaks for care.`,answer:!0},{text:`The text suggests that careers are now seen as less linear than in the past.`,answer:!0},{text:`All recruiters have abandoned their negative view of gaps in employment history.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-062`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the debate moved 'from the margins to the mainstream', curators claimed origin countries 'lacked the facilities to conserve them', national laws can stop 'even a willing director', and few expect the old position 'to survive'.`,passage:`Museums across Europe are grappling with demands to return objects acquired during the colonial era, and the debate has moved with remarkable speed from the margins to the mainstream. A generation ago, curators routinely dismissed restitution claims on the grounds that their institutions held items in trust for humanity and that the countries of origin lacked the facilities to conserve them. Both arguments have worn thin. Several of the claimant nations have built modern museums of their own, and the language of universal stewardship sounds increasingly hollow when the objects in question were seized during military expeditions. Some institutions have now handed back sculptures, manuscripts and ceremonial items, often amid considerable public ceremony. Others remain constrained by national laws that forbid the disposal of state collections, so that even a willing director cannot act without new legislation. The process is not without its complications. Disagreements occasionally arise about which modern community is the rightful recipient, particularly where historical kingdoms have been divided by later borders. Even so, the direction of travel seems clear, and few observers expect the old position, that possession is effectively permanent, to survive the coming decade.`,statements:[{text:`Restitution has only recently become a mainstream topic of debate.`,answer:!0},{text:`Curators in the past argued that countries of origin could not properly care for the objects.`,answer:!0},{text:`Every museum director who wishes to return objects is free to do so immediately.`,answer:!1},{text:`The author expects museums to keep their colonial collections permanently.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-063`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says staff had 'no reduction in pay', most firms 'chose to continue', the companies 'volunteered to take part', and sectors like hospitals cannot compress work 'without hiring additional staff'.`,passage:`The four-day working week has graduated from a utopian fantasy to a subject of serious experiment. In a series of trials involving dozens of companies, employees were given an extra day off each week with no reduction in pay, on condition that they maintained their previous output. The results, by most measures, were striking. A large majority of participating firms chose to continue with the arrangement after the trial ended, citing improved morale, fewer sick days and, in many cases, an easier time attracting staff. Productivity, which many managers had feared would slump, held steady or rose modestly in most organisations. Enthusiasts have hailed these findings as proof that the five-day week is an industrial relic. More cautious observers point out that the companies concerned volunteered to take part, and were therefore likely to be those best suited to the model. Sectors in which output depends on hours of physical presence, such as hospitals, care homes and public transport, cannot simply compress their work into fewer days without hiring additional staff. For them, a shorter week would carry costs that the existing trials, conducted largely in offices, have not had to confront.`,statements:[{text:`Workers in the trials kept their full salaries despite working fewer days.`,answer:!0},{text:`Most participating companies returned to a five-day week once the trial ended.`,answer:!1},{text:`The companies in the trials were chosen at random.`,answer:!1},{text:`The author suggests the results may not apply easily to hospitals or transport.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-064`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says tomatoes were first 'viewed with suspicion', chillies, potatoes and maize also came from abroad, historians say this does not make dishes 'any less authentic', and some chefs complain while others welcome the rules.`,passage:`Food historians have a habit of puncturing cherished national myths, and few dishes have escaped their scrutiny. Consider the widely held belief that a particular cuisine has remained essentially unchanged for centuries. In reality, many ingredients now regarded as quintessentially traditional are relatively recent arrivals. The tomato, for example, reached Europe from the Americas in the sixteenth century and was initially viewed with suspicion, even grown as an ornamental plant, before it became indispensable in southern kitchens. Chillies, potatoes and maize followed similar trajectories, transforming diets on several continents within a few generations. None of this, historians insist, makes the dishes any less authentic. Authenticity, they argue, is better understood as a matter of continuity of practice than of fixed origins: a recipe becomes traditional once a community has adopted it, adapted it and passed it on. The point has practical consequences. Regional producers who seek legal protection for their specialities must define what makes them distinctive, and overly rigid definitions can freeze a living tradition in a single, arbitrary moment. Some chefs complain that such rules stifle innovation, while others welcome them as a bulwark against cheap industrial imitations.`,statements:[{text:`Tomatoes were immediately popular as a food when they arrived in Europe.`,answer:!1},{text:`Several ingredients now seen as traditional came from the Americas.`,answer:!0},{text:`Historians argue that recently adopted ingredients make a dish inauthentic.`,answer:!1},{text:`Chefs disagree about the value of legal protection for regional foods.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-065`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the promise 'has largely been kept' for some, algorithms penalise 'declining jobs', contractors have had 'no entitlement' to holiday pay, and courts have ruled workers are 'more akin to employees'.`,passage:`The gig economy was originally marketed as a liberation: workers would choose their own hours, answer to no boss and fit paid tasks around the rest of their lives. For some, particularly students and those seeking a second income, the promise has largely been kept. For others, the reality has proved considerably less rosy. Drivers and couriers who depend on app-based platforms for their entire livelihood often find that the algorithms allocating work exert a form of control every bit as demanding as a human supervisor, penalising them for declining jobs or logging off at busy times. Because they are typically classified as self-employed contractors, they have historically had no entitlement to sick pay, holiday pay or a minimum wage. Courts in several countries have begun to challenge this classification, ruling that the degree of control exercised by the platforms makes their workers more akin to employees. The companies counter that reclassification would force them to restrict flexibility, raise prices and ultimately reduce the number of jobs available. The outcome of these battles will help determine whether the gig economy evolves into a regulated industry or remains a grey zone between employment and enterprise.`,statements:[{text:`The text claims that gig work has disappointed every type of worker.`,answer:!1},{text:`App algorithms can punish workers for refusing jobs.`,answer:!0},{text:`Gig workers have traditionally received holiday pay from the platforms.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some courts have decided that platform workers resemble employees.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-066`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says visitor numbers 'fell steadily' with online searching, being free of spending is the 'most precious asset', budgets 'have been squeezed', and librarians 'worry' about volunteers eroding expertise.`,passage:`Public libraries, written off a decade ago as relics of a pre-digital age, have staged an unlikely revival in many towns. Visitor numbers, which fell steadily as online searching became ubiquitous, have recovered in places where libraries have reinvented themselves as all-purpose community hubs. Alongside books, they now lend tools, musical instruments and laptops; they host job clubs, language classes and homework sessions; and they offer warm, quiet spaces where no one is expected to spend money. That final point, librarians stress, has become their most precious asset. As cafés, shopping centres and even parks have become more commercial or more heavily policed, the library remains one of the few indoor places where a person can simply exist without being a customer. The revival is fragile, however. Funding is usually controlled by local authorities whose budgets have been squeezed for years, and many branches survive only because volunteers have taken over tasks once performed by paid staff. Professional librarians worry that this arrangement, though well-intentioned, erodes expertise and allows politicians to claim that services have been preserved while their substance quietly drains away.`,statements:[{text:`Library visits declined as internet use became widespread.`,answer:!0},{text:`Librarians consider the absence of pressure to spend money a key advantage.`,answer:!0},{text:`Library funding has increased significantly in recent years.`,answer:!1},{text:`Professional librarians are entirely happy with the growing reliance on volunteers.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-067`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says correct decisions 'have risen markedly', the emotional cost 'was not foreseen', offside was meant to stop attackers 'loitering near goal', and fans in the ground are 'left in the dark'.`,passage:`Few sporting innovations have provoked as much anguish as video review in football. Its advocates promised an end to the glaring injustices that had decided matches and even tournaments, and on the narrow question of accuracy they have largely been vindicated: the proportion of correct decisions on goals, penalties and dismissals has risen markedly. The emotional cost, however, was not foreseen. Fans in the stadium now hesitate before celebrating a goal, aware that it may be disallowed minutes later for an infringement invisible to the naked eye. Offside decisions measured in centimetres have struck many as a perversion of the rule's original purpose, which was to prevent attackers from loitering near goal rather than to adjudicate on the position of a toenail. Supporters inside the ground also complain that they are left in the dark, unable to see the replays that viewers at home are watching. Some leagues have responded by allowing referees to explain their decisions over the public-address system, a practice borrowed from other sports. Purists remain unconvinced, arguing that a game once valued for its flow and spontaneity has been subordinated to the pursuit of an unattainable perfection.`,statements:[{text:`Video review has increased the number of correct refereeing decisions.`,answer:!0},{text:`The negative effect on fans' celebrations was predicted from the start.`,answer:!1},{text:`The original aim of the offside rule was to stop attackers waiting near the goal.`,answer:!0},{text:`Fans in the stadium can usually see the same replays as television viewers.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-068`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text sets out three camps, says economists 'disagree sharply about their relative weight', states cheap credit pushed prices up 'without adding a single brick', and says governments favour the remedy 'least likely to upset' homeowners.`,passage:`Whenever house prices climb faster than wages, a familiar argument breaks out over the culprit. One camp blames restrictive planning rules, which make it slow and costly to build new homes and thus keep supply chronically short of demand. Another points to cheap credit, arguing that years of low interest rates allowed buyers to borrow ever larger sums, which simply pushed prices upward without adding a single brick. A third highlights the role of property as an investment vehicle, noting that homes are increasingly bought not to be lived in but to generate rent or capital gains. In truth, most economists accept that all three forces interact, though they disagree sharply about their relative weight. The disagreement is not merely academic, because each diagnosis implies a different cure. If supply is the main problem, the answer is to build more; if credit is to blame, tighter lending rules are needed; if speculation is the driver, taxes on second homes and empty properties become more attractive. Governments, unsurprisingly, tend to favour whichever remedy is least likely to upset existing homeowners, who constitute a large and reliable share of the electorate.`,statements:[{text:`According to one view, cheap borrowing raised prices without increasing the number of homes.`,answer:!0},{text:`The text presents three different explanations for rising house prices.`,answer:!0},{text:`Economists agree on which factor matters most.`,answer:!1},{text:`Governments usually choose the policy that homeowners dislike most.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-069`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text mentions ministers 'with responsibility for social connection', says the problem 'is not confined to the elderly', states living alone 'does not in itself cause harm', and says the evidence 'is still accumulating'.`,passage:`Loneliness, once considered a private misfortune, is now treated by many public-health officials as a problem on a par with smoking or obesity. Large studies have linked chronic isolation to higher rates of heart disease, dementia and early death, prompting several governments to appoint ministers with responsibility for social connection. Contrary to popular assumption, the problem is not confined to the elderly. Surveys repeatedly find that young adults report feeling lonely at least as often as pensioners, possibly because the transitions of early adulthood, such as leaving home, starting university or moving for work, disrupt established friendships. Researchers are careful to distinguish loneliness from solitude. Living alone, or spending long periods without company, does not in itself cause harm; what matters is the painful gap between the relationships a person has and those they would like to have. This distinction complicates policy. Initiatives that simply bring people into the same room, such as lunch clubs or community events, help some participants but leave others untouched, since the quality of a connection counts for far more than its frequency. Doctors in some regions can now 'prescribe' activities such as gardening groups or choirs, though the evidence for such schemes is still accumulating.`,statements:[{text:`Some governments have given ministers responsibility for tackling isolation.`,answer:!0},{text:`Loneliness affects older people almost exclusively.`,answer:!1},{text:`Researchers believe that spending time alone is always harmful.`,answer:!1},{text:`The effectiveness of 'social prescribing' has not yet been fully established.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-070`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text calls juries 'a vital safeguard against an overbearing state', mentions proposals for fraud cases to be heard 'by a judge sitting alone', says laws 'prohibit jurors from disclosing' deliberations, and polls show 'strong public support'.`,passage:`Jury service is often described as one of the last remaining obligations of citizenship, alongside paying taxes and, in some countries, military service. The principle behind it is that ordinary people, rather than professional judges alone, should decide whether a fellow citizen is guilty of a serious crime. Defenders regard this as a vital safeguard against an overbearing state: twelve strangers, they argue, are far harder to pressure than a single official. Critics raise a number of objections. Complex fraud trials can last for months and involve technical evidence that even trained accountants struggle to follow, leading some to propose that such cases be heard by a judge sitting alone or with expert assessors. Others worry that jurors, despite strict instructions, may be swayed by what they read online about a defendant. Research into jury behaviour is hampered by laws in many jurisdictions that prohibit jurors from disclosing what was said during their deliberations. Studies have therefore relied heavily on mock trials, whose participants know that nothing real is at stake. Despite these reservations, opinion polls consistently show strong public support for the jury system, which retains a symbolic weight out of all proportion to the number of cases it actually decides.`,statements:[{text:`Supporters believe juries protect citizens against excessive state power.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some critics suggest that long fraud trials should be decided without a jury.`,answer:!0},{text:`Jurors are always free to tell researchers what was said during their deliberations.`,answer:!1},{text:`Public opinion has turned strongly against the jury system.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-071`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text links European street food to 'dubious hygiene', says some vendors 'have even been awarded' restaurant distinctions, notes stalls avoid 'crippling overheads', and says relocation was 'praised' but also 'lamented'.`,passage:`Street food occupies a curious position in the culinary hierarchy. In many Asian and Latin American cities it is the backbone of everyday eating, valued for its speed, cheapness and astonishing variety, while in parts of Europe it was long associated with greasy late-night snacks of dubious hygiene. That perception has shifted dramatically. Covered markets that once sold only fresh produce now house dozens of stalls offering everything from hand-pulled noodles to wood-fired flatbreads, and some vendors have even been awarded prestigious culinary distinctions normally reserved for restaurants. The appeal lies partly in informality. Diners can sample several cuisines in a single visit without the expense or ceremony of a formal meal, and young cooks can test an idea without the crippling overheads of a permanent restaurant lease. Yet success brings its own pressures. As markets become fashionable, rents for stalls tend to rise, squeezing out the very traders whose authenticity attracted the crowds in the first place. In some Asian cities, meanwhile, authorities have relocated hawkers into regulated food centres in the name of public health, a policy praised for improving standards but lamented by those who feel it has drained street life of its spontaneity.`,statements:[{text:`In parts of Europe, street food used to have a poor reputation.`,answer:!0},{text:`No street-food vendor has ever received an award usually given to restaurants.`,answer:!1},{text:`Running a stall usually costs more than running a permanent restaurant.`,answer:!1},{text:`Moving hawkers into regulated centres has produced mixed reactions.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-072`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says courses were meant to 'democratise higher education', completion rates are 'often in single figures', finishers 'already hold a degree', and engagement depends 'less' on content than on belonging.`,passage:`Universities have embraced online learning with an enthusiasm that would have been unthinkable twenty years ago. Lectures are routinely recorded, entire degree programmes are delivered remotely, and so-called massive open online courses attract hundreds of thousands of registrations. The initial hope was that such courses would democratise higher education, bringing elite teaching to anyone with an internet connection. That ambition has been only partially realised. Completion rates for free online courses are notoriously low, often in single figures, and the learners who do finish tend to be those who already hold a degree. In other words, the technology has so far served mainly to enrich the education of the already educated. This should not obscure its genuine benefits. Mature students juggling jobs and families can now study at hours that suit them, and recorded lectures allow learners with disabilities or limited language skills to revisit material at their own pace. The more difficult lesson concerns motivation. Educators increasingly recognise that what keeps students engaged is less the quality of the content than the sense of belonging to a community, complete with deadlines, discussion and the knowledge that someone will notice if they disappear.`,statements:[{text:`Educators now believe content quality matters more than a sense of community.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most people who sign up for free online courses complete them.`,answer:!1},{text:`Online courses were originally expected to widen access to higher education.`,answer:!0},{text:`Those who finish such courses are often already graduates.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-073`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the process begins with 'artists and students' and ends with chain stores, owners 'see the value of their homes soar', scholars 'disagree' about displacement, and land trusts are listed among the experiments.`,passage:`The gentrification of a neighbourhood rarely announces itself with fanfare. It tends to begin with artists and students drawn by low rents, followed by independent cafés and galleries, then estate agents, and finally the chain stores and luxury flats that signal the process is complete. For longstanding residents, the experience is profoundly ambivalent. Streets become cleaner and safer, empty shops reopen and property owners see the value of their homes soar. Tenants, however, frequently face rent increases that force them to leave the very area whose character they helped to create. Urban scholars disagree about how widespread such displacement really is. Some studies have found that poorer residents in gentrifying districts move out no more often than those elsewhere, suggesting that the change occurs mainly through who moves in rather than who is pushed out. Others argue that these statistics miss subtler forms of loss, such as the closure of affordable shops, the disappearance of familiar meeting places and the sense of being a stranger in one's own street. Policymakers searching for a middle path have experimented with rent controls, community land trusts and requirements that new developments include a share of affordable housing.`,statements:[{text:`According to the text, gentrification usually begins with the arrival of chain stores.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some homeowners benefit financially from gentrification.`,answer:!0},{text:`All studies agree that poorer residents are forced out in large numbers.`,answer:!1},{text:`Community land trusts are mentioned as one possible policy response.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-074`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says participation 'rose dramatically', reviews found effects 'considerably smaller', disappointing experiments were 'less likely to be published', and the hope that nudges could replace regulation 'looks misplaced'.`,passage:`Behavioural economics has popularised the idea that small changes in how choices are presented can have large effects on what people decide. Placing healthy food at eye level in a canteen, sending tax reminders that mention how many neighbours have already paid, or enrolling employees in a pension scheme automatically unless they opt out: such 'nudges' have been adopted by governments eager for cheap and politically painless reforms. The pension example is the most celebrated. When enrolment became the default, participation rates in several countries rose dramatically, far more than any advertising campaign had achieved. Recently, however, the field has faced uncomfortable questions. Large reviews have suggested that the average effect of nudges is considerably smaller than early, eye-catching studies implied, partly because experiments with disappointing results were less likely to be published. Critics also raise ethical concerns, arguing that steering people without their conscious awareness sits uneasily with respect for individual autonomy. Defenders reply that every choice is presented in some way, so there is no neutral option; the only question is whether the design serves people's interests. Few dispute that nudges have a place, but the hope that they could substitute for regulation now looks misplaced.`,statements:[{text:`Automatic pension enrolment greatly increased participation in several countries.`,answer:!0},{text:`Recent reviews suggest nudges are more powerful than early studies claimed.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some studies with weak results may never have been published.`,answer:!0},{text:`The author believes nudges can fully replace regulation.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-075`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says companies 'settled for hybrid arrangements', many staff would 'accept a pay cut' to keep flexibility, managers worry about lost 'informal mentoring', and conversion is 'more technically complex than it sounds'.`,passage:`Remote work, adopted almost overnight by millions of office employees during the pandemic, has proved remarkably difficult to reverse. Many companies that issued confident predictions about a full return to the office have quietly settled for hybrid arrangements, in which staff spend two or three days a week at their desks. Surveys indicate that employees value the flexibility so highly that a significant proportion would accept a pay cut rather than give it up. Managers' attitudes are more divided. Some report that productivity has held up well, particularly for focused individual tasks. Others worry about less tangible losses: the informal mentoring that junior staff once absorbed by overhearing experienced colleagues, the chance encounters that spark new ideas, and the gradual weakening of loyalty to an organisation experienced mainly through a screen. The effects ripple far beyond the workplace. City-centre sandwich shops and dry cleaners that depended on commuters have suffered, while suburban high streets and small towns within reach of major cities have enjoyed a modest revival. Commercial landlords, meanwhile, face a glut of empty office space, and some planners are exploring whether redundant tower blocks could be converted into housing, a process more technically complex than it sounds.`,statements:[{text:`Converting office blocks into homes is described as a simple process.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many firms have accepted hybrid working rather than a full return to the office.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some managers fear that junior employees learn less when working remotely.`,answer:!0},{text:`Surveys suggest that employees place little value on flexibility.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-076`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says some hosts transformed 'derelict industrial land', economists agree the Games 'rarely pay for themselves', the number of bidders 'has fallen sharply', and events may now be spread 'across several cities' using existing venues.`,passage:`The Olympic Games have long been sold to host cities as a catalyst for regeneration. Bids promise new transport links, rejuvenated districts and a lasting boost to tourism and civic pride. The historical record, however, is decidedly mixed. Some hosts have used the Games to transform derelict industrial land into thriving neighbourhoods, and a few still benefit from infrastructure that might otherwise have taken decades to build. Others have been left with crippling debts and abandoned stadiums slowly crumbling on the city's outskirts, monuments to a fortnight of glory. Economists who have studied the subject are almost unanimous that the Games rarely pay for themselves in narrow financial terms; costs routinely overrun initial estimates, sometimes several times over. As a result, the number of cities willing to bid has fallen sharply, and in several cases referendums have rejected candidacies outright. Organisers have responded by encouraging the use of existing or temporary venues and by allowing events to be spread across several cities or even countries. Whether these reforms can restore the Games' appeal remains uncertain, but they represent a striking admission that the old model of building lavishly from scratch is no longer politically sustainable.`,statements:[{text:`Economists generally agree that the Games are financially profitable.`,answer:!1},{text:`Fewer cities are now prepared to bid to host the Games.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some host cities have successfully redeveloped former industrial areas.`,answer:!0},{text:`Organisers now insist that all events take place in one city with new venues.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-077`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text describes a 'temporary monopoly', critics say extensions benefit 'corporations' more than living artists, orphan works are those 'whose owners cannot be traced', and courts 'may well reach conflicting conclusions'.`,passage:`Copyright law was designed for a world in which copying required printing presses, recording studios or at least a photocopier. Its central bargain was simple: creators received a temporary monopoly over their work in exchange for its eventual passage into the public domain, where anyone could use it freely. Over the past century, however, that monopoly has been extended repeatedly, and in many countries it now lasts for seventy years after the author's death. Critics argue that these extensions benefit corporations holding valuable back catalogues far more than living artists, and that they lock up vast quantities of 'orphan works' whose owners cannot be traced, preventing libraries and archives from digitising them. Defenders counter that strong protection is essential in an age when a song or a film can be duplicated and distributed worldwide in seconds. The rise of generative software, trained on enormous collections of existing texts and images, has added a new layer of controversy. Authors and illustrators have launched lawsuits claiming that their work was used without permission or payment, while technology firms maintain that training constitutes a lawful form of analysis. Courts on different continents may well reach conflicting conclusions, leaving creators and developers alike in a state of uncertainty.`,statements:[{text:`Copyright was originally intended to last for a limited period.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics believe longer copyright terms mainly help living artists.`,answer:!1},{text:`Orphan works are works whose owners cannot be found.`,answer:!0},{text:`Courts around the world have already agreed on how to treat generative software.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-078`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the family meal is 'mourned' as declining, shared meals among the poor were 'far from guaranteed', the ideal owes much to 'its portrayal in advertising', and researchers 'caution against assuming' the meal is the cause.`,passage:`The tradition of the family meal, with parents and children gathered around a table at a fixed hour, is frequently mourned as a casualty of modern life. Commentators blame long working hours, extracurricular activities and the lure of screens for its supposed decline. Yet the historical evidence complicates this narrative. In many households of the past, particularly among the working poor, shared meals were far from guaranteed; shift patterns, cramped housing and the sheer labour of cooking meant that family members often ate at different times and in different places. The idealised dinner table owes a good deal to the middle-class domestic culture of the mid-twentieth century and to its portrayal in advertising. This does not mean that shared meals are unimportant. Researchers have found associations between regular family dinners and outcomes such as better diet and stronger vocabulary among children. However, they caution against assuming that the meal itself is the cause. Families who eat together frequently may differ in many other respects, including income and the amount of time parents have available, and it may be these underlying advantages rather than the act of dining that make the difference.`,statements:[{text:`Historical evidence shows that poor families in the past always ate together.`,answer:!1},{text:`Researchers have proved that eating together directly causes better outcomes for children.`,answer:!1},{text:`Advertising helped shape the image of the ideal family dinner.`,answer:!0},{text:`Commentators often claim that family meals are becoming less common.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-079`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says inflation erodes cash savings, fixed-rate borrowers see their debts 'shrink', deflation makes consumers 'delay purchases', and central banks were criticised 'for reacting too slowly'.`,passage:`Inflation is often described as a hidden tax, and the description is apt in more ways than one. When prices rise steadily, the real value of savings held in cash is eroded, while borrowers with fixed-rate loans find that their debts shrink relative to their incomes. The effects are therefore deeply uneven. Pensioners living on fixed incomes and households with modest savings tend to suffer most, whereas governments and homeowners with large mortgages may quietly benefit. Central banks in most advanced economies aim for an inflation rate of around two per cent, a figure that strikes many people as arbitrary. Economists defend it on several grounds. A small positive rate gives central banks room to cut interest rates during a downturn, and it reduces the danger of deflation, a spiral of falling prices in which consumers delay purchases in the expectation that goods will become cheaper still. Measured inflation can also overstate the true rise in living costs, because official indices struggle to capture improvements in the quality of products. When inflation surged unexpectedly after the pandemic, central banks were criticised for reacting too slowly, having initially dismissed the rise as a temporary consequence of disrupted supply chains.`,statements:[{text:`Central banks responded very quickly to the inflation that followed the pandemic.`,answer:!1},{text:`Inflation reduces the real value of savings kept in cash.`,answer:!0},{text:`People who owe money on fixed-rate loans are usually harmed by inflation.`,answer:!1},{text:`Deflation can lead consumers to postpone buying things.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-080`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the proportion of top marks 'has risen substantially', 'not everyone accepts' the slipping-standards view, there is 'no reason in principle' why all should not get the top grade, and critics fear more 'competition and anxiety'.`,passage:`Grading has always been a contentious business, but debates about grade inflation have grown particularly heated in recent years. In many education systems, the proportion of students awarded the highest marks has risen substantially over two decades, prompting accusations that standards have slipped. Universities complain that they can no longer distinguish between applicants, and employers grumble that a top degree no longer guarantees exceptional ability. Not everyone accepts this interpretation. Some educators argue that teaching has genuinely improved, that students are better prepared for examinations than their predecessors, and that rising grades partly reflect a welcome shift from comparing students against one another towards measuring them against fixed criteria. Under such a system, there is no reason in principle why every candidate should not achieve the top grade if they reach the required standard. The difficulty is that public confidence depends on grades carrying a stable meaning over time. Some institutions have therefore introduced supplementary measures, such as detailed transcripts showing a student's rank within their cohort or the distribution of marks in each course. Critics fear these innovations will intensify competition and anxiety among students who already feel under relentless pressure to perform.`,statements:[{text:`The share of top grades has increased considerably over the past two decades.`,answer:!0},{text:`All educators accept that rising grades prove standards have fallen.`,answer:!1},{text:`Under criteria-based assessment, every student could in theory receive the highest grade.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics believe that publishing rankings will reduce student stress.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-081`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says audiences 'rival those of traditional sporting finals', the status of esports 'remains a matter of lively debate', many players retire 'in their mid-twenties', and no football federation has 'ever possessed' such control.`,passage:`Esports, the competitive playing of video games, has grown from a niche hobby into a global industry with professional leagues, lucrative sponsorship deals and arenas packed with cheering spectators. Major tournaments now draw online audiences that rival those of traditional sporting finals, and top players earn salaries that would have seemed fantastical a generation ago. Whether esports deserve to be called sport at all remains a matter of lively debate. Sceptics point to the absence of significant physical exertion, arguing that sport by definition involves the body as much as the mind. Advocates respond that elite players display extraordinary reaction speeds, hand-eye coordination and strategic thinking, and that their training regimes, sometimes involving ten hours of practice a day, are every bit as gruelling as those in more conventional disciplines. The industry nonetheless faces serious challenges. Careers are notoriously short, with many players retiring in their mid-twenties because of burnout or repetitive strain injuries. Moreover, the games themselves are owned by private companies, which can alter the rules, withdraw a title or shut down a league at will, a degree of control no football federation has ever possessed over the sport it governs.`,statements:[{text:`Football federations have the same control over their sport as games companies have over esports.`,answer:!1},{text:`Everyone now agrees that esports are a genuine sport.`,answer:!1},{text:`Professional esports careers often end relatively early.`,answer:!0},{text:`Online audiences for big esports tournaments can be as large as those for traditional sports finals.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-082`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says managers may spend 'more than half their working week' in meetings, the problem is 'not meetings as such', fragmented days hurt those who 'need to concentrate', and enthusiasm 'tends to fade'.`,passage:`Few aspects of office life generate as much quiet resentment as meetings. Studies of corporate time use suggest that senior managers may spend more than half their working week in them, and a substantial share of attendees confess to answering emails or daydreaming while others speak. The problem, organisational psychologists argue, is not meetings as such but the casual way in which they are convened. Calendars make it effortless to invite a dozen colleagues to an hour-long discussion, and few people feel able to decline an invitation from a superior. As a result, meetings expand to fill the available time, decisions are deferred to follow-up sessions, and the people who actually need to concentrate find their days fragmented into useless half-hour slivers. Some companies have experimented with radical remedies, such as banning recurring meetings altogether, declaring one day a week free of them, or requiring that every invitation be accompanied by a written agenda and a clear statement of the decision to be made. The results suggest that such measures can reclaim considerable time, although enthusiasm tends to fade as old habits creep back. Lasting change, researchers conclude, depends less on rules than on leaders who visibly protect their own time.`,statements:[{text:`The benefits of anti-meeting rules usually last indefinitely.`,answer:!1},{text:`Fragmented schedules make it harder for some employees to concentrate.`,answer:!0},{text:`Some senior managers spend over half their working week in meetings.`,answer:!0},{text:`Psychologists argue that meetings are inherently useless.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-083`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says young people use it 'regardless of their family origins', speakers 'switch to more conventional forms' formally, linguists call the varieties 'rule-governed systems', and standard features 'began life in exactly the same way'.`,passage:`Cities have always been laboratories of language. When large numbers of people from different linguistic backgrounds live in close proximity, new ways of speaking inevitably emerge, and linguists have documented a striking example in the multicultural districts of several European capitals. Young people there, regardless of their family origins, have developed a shared style of speech that blends the dominant national language with words, sounds and grammatical patterns drawn from the languages of immigrant communities. Contrary to the alarmist claims sometimes made in the press, this is not evidence of declining standards. Speakers typically switch to more conventional forms in formal settings such as job interviews, demonstrating a sophisticated awareness of register. Linguists stress that such varieties are rule-governed systems, as consistent in their internal logic as any standard dialect. They also point out that many features now regarded as respectable in the standard language began life in exactly the same way, as markers of a particular group that gradually spread. Nevertheless, speakers of these urban varieties report facing prejudice, with teachers or employers sometimes interpreting their speech as a sign of low intelligence, an assumption for which there is no linguistic basis whatsoever.`,statements:[{text:`The new urban speech style is used only by young people from immigrant families.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many speakers adapt their language in formal situations.`,answer:!0},{text:`Linguists describe these varieties as lacking consistent rules.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some features of standard languages originated in particular social groups.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-084`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says one review 'could empty a dining room', ratings have 'well-documented flaws', reviews come mostly from those with 'exceptionally good or exceptionally bad' experiences, and owners 'still prize' professional reviews.`,passage:`Restaurant critics once wielded enormous power. A single damning review in a national newspaper could empty a dining room within weeks, while a rave could create queues around the block. The rise of online review platforms has diluted that influence considerably, spreading it among millions of amateur reviewers whose collective verdicts are summarised in a star rating. In theory, this is a triumph of democracy over elitism. In practice, the system has well-documented flaws. Ratings are vulnerable to manipulation, whether through fake positive reviews purchased by owners or coordinated attacks by competitors and disgruntled customers. Research has also shown that reviews are disproportionately written by people who had either an exceptionally good or an exceptionally bad experience, so the average rating may say little about a typical visit. Professional critics, for their part, argue that their value lies not in a simple verdict but in context: the ability to situate a meal within culinary traditions, to explain why a dish succeeds or fails, and to write prose that readers enjoy for its own sake. Many restaurateurs admit that they still prize a thoughtful professional review, even as they anxiously monitor their ratings online every morning.`,statements:[{text:`A single review by a professional critic could once have a major impact on a restaurant.`,answer:!0},{text:`Online ratings are described as completely reliable.`,answer:!1},{text:`Customers with average experiences write most online reviews.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many restaurant owners still value reviews by professional critics.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-085`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the textbook view expected employers to 'hire fewer workers', 1990s studies found 'little or no evidence of job losses', some argue employers have 'bargaining power', and risks 'grow' as the minimum rises.`,passage:`Minimum wage laws have been the subject of fierce economic debate for well over a century. The traditional textbook prediction was straightforward: if the government forces employers to pay more than the market rate for low-skilled labour, they will hire fewer workers, and unemployment among precisely the people the law was meant to help will rise. During the 1990s, however, a series of influential studies compared neighbouring regions with different minimum wages and found little or no evidence of job losses following increases. These findings prompted a lively reassessment. Some economists concluded that many employers possess enough bargaining power to hold wages below what a competitive market would produce, in which case a modest legal floor can raise pay without reducing employment. Others remained unconvinced, arguing that the effects may take years to appear, perhaps as firms gradually invest in machines that replace workers. Today the consensus, insofar as one exists, is that moderate increases have small effects on jobs, but that the risks grow as the minimum approaches a large share of the typical local wage. The difficulty lies in knowing exactly where that threshold lies in any particular economy.`,statements:[{text:`Traditional theory predicted that minimum wages would reduce employment.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies in the 1990s found large job losses after minimum wage increases.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some economists think employers often have the power to keep wages artificially low.`,answer:!0},{text:`Economists agree that very high minimum wages carry no risks.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-086`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says volunteering is most common among the 'well educated, financially secure', informal help 'often goes unrecorded', poorer areas 'may sustain extensive networks', and charities are trying 'flexible, short-term roles'.`,passage:`Volunteering is usually praised as an unambiguous good, a source of civic vitality that benefits both the helper and the helped. Sociologists, however, have begun to ask awkward questions about who volunteers and why. Survey data consistently show that formal volunteering is most common among people who are well educated, financially secure and already embedded in dense social networks. Those on low incomes, working irregular hours or caring for relatives have far less time to give, and their informal help to neighbours and relatives often goes unrecorded in official statistics. The result is a distorted picture in which affluent areas appear rich in community spirit and poorer ones seem barren, when in fact the latter may sustain extensive networks of mutual support that simply do not take the form of registered organisations. The distinction matters because governments increasingly rely on volunteers to deliver services, from running libraries to visiting isolated pensioners. If such services depend on a supply of unpaid labour that is concentrated in prosperous districts, they may end up reinforcing the very inequalities they were intended to reduce. Some charities are now experimenting with flexible, short-term roles designed to suit people whose lives leave little room for regular commitments.`,statements:[{text:`Official statistics fully record the informal help given in poorer communities.`,answer:!1},{text:`Charities are introducing longer, more demanding volunteer roles.`,answer:!1},{text:`Formal volunteering is most common among wealthier, better-educated people.`,answer:!0},{text:`The author suggests that poorer areas may have more community support than they appear to.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-087`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says these foods were 'familiar mainly to the communities' that made them, evidence of health outcomes 'remains limited', pasteurisation 'kills the very live cultures', and the author says they need not 'be avoided'.`,passage:`The rise of fermented foods from obscure health-shop curiosities to supermarket staples has been one of the more surprising culinary trends of recent years. Kimchi, kefir, kombucha and sauerkraut, once familiar mainly to the communities that had produced them for generations, now fill whole refrigerated aisles. Much of the enthusiasm stems from growing scientific interest in the gut microbiome, the trillions of bacteria living in the human digestive tract that are thought to influence everything from digestion to mood. Marketing departments have seized on this research with predictable zeal, adorning labels with claims about immunity and wellbeing. Scientists are noticeably more restrained. While some studies suggest that a diet rich in fermented foods can increase the diversity of gut bacteria, which is generally considered beneficial, evidence that these changes translate into concrete health outcomes remains limited. Moreover, many commercial products are pasteurised after fermentation to extend their shelf life, a process that kills the very live cultures consumers believe they are paying for. None of this means that fermented foods should be avoided; they are often delicious and nutritious. But shoppers would be wise to treat the more extravagant promises on the packaging with a healthy dose of scepticism.`,statements:[{text:`Scientists have proved that fermented foods significantly improve health outcomes.`,answer:!1},{text:`The author advises people to stop eating fermented foods.`,answer:!1},{text:`Pasteurisation can destroy the live bacteria in some products.`,answer:!0},{text:`Fermented foods were once known mainly to the communities that traditionally made them.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-088`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says mediation is 'far cheaper', proceedings are 'confidential rather than public', power imbalance makes it unsuitable, and critics 'worry' that it 'cannot adequately protect' vulnerable people.`,passage:`Mediation, in which a neutral third party helps disputing sides reach their own agreement, has expanded rapidly as an alternative to litigation. Its advantages are easy to list. It is generally quicker and far cheaper than going to court, proceedings are confidential rather than public, and because the parties craft the outcome themselves, they are more likely to comply with it. In family disputes, where former partners may need to cooperate over children for years to come, the ability to preserve a working relationship is particularly valuable. Some jurisdictions now require couples to attend an information session on mediation before they can begin court proceedings over child arrangements. Yet the process is not suitable for every case. Where there is a serious imbalance of power between the parties, as in relationships marked by domestic abuse, sitting across a table from the other side can be intimidating or even dangerous, and the weaker party may feel pressured into accepting unfair terms. Mediators are trained to recognise such situations, and exemptions from mandatory sessions usually apply. Critics nonetheless worry that the drive to reduce court backlogs and legal-aid spending is pushing vulnerable people towards a process that cannot adequately protect them.`,statements:[{text:`Mediation is usually less expensive than going to court.`,answer:!0},{text:`Mediation sessions are normally held in public.`,answer:!1},{text:`Mediation may be inappropriate when one party has much more power than the other.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics are confident that vulnerable people are fully protected in mediation.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-089`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text compares handwriting to 'a slide rule', says students taking notes by hand 'remember lectures better', some replications 'produced weaker effects', and typing can be 'transformative' for pupils with motor difficulties.`,passage:`The question of whether schools should teach handwriting in an era of keyboards and touchscreens has divided educators. Some regard joined-up writing as an anachronism comparable to teaching children to use a slide rule, arguing that classroom hours would be better spent on coding or digital literacy. Others maintain that handwriting confers cognitive benefits that typing does not. Several experiments have found that students who take notes by hand remember lectures better than those who type, possibly because the slower pace of writing forces them to summarise and rephrase rather than transcribe word for word. Brain-imaging studies of young children suggest that forming letters by hand activates regions associated with reading more strongly than pressing keys does. The evidence, though suggestive, is not conclusive; some attempts to replicate the note-taking findings have produced weaker effects. There are also practical considerations. Examinations in many countries are still written on paper, and pupils who struggle to write quickly and legibly are at a disadvantage regardless of what they know. For pupils with motor difficulties, however, being permitted to type can be transformative, allowing their understanding to be assessed rather than their dexterity.`,statements:[{text:`Some educators compare teaching joined-up writing to teaching an outdated tool.`,answer:!0},{text:`Typing is described as unhelpful for pupils with motor difficulties.`,answer:!1},{text:`Experiments show that typed notes always lead to better memory of lectures.`,answer:!1},{text:`Attempts to repeat the note-taking studies have not all produced equally strong results.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-090`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says engineers 'covered them with concrete', the project involved 'demolishing an elevated motorway', exposed water 'helps to cool surrounding streets', and critics say some rely on water 'pumped in artificially'.`,passage:`In many cities, the river that gave the settlement its reason for existing was eventually buried. As industrialisation turned waterways into open sewers, engineers covered them with concrete, diverted them into pipes and built roads and car parks on top. Out of sight, these streams were soon out of mind. Over the past three decades, however, a movement known as 'daylighting' has sought to bring such rivers back to the surface. The most celebrated project, in an East Asian capital, involved demolishing an elevated motorway and restoring a long-hidden stream that now runs through a landscaped park several kilometres long. The benefits claimed for these schemes are considerable. Exposed water helps to cool surrounding streets during heatwaves, provides habitats for fish and birds, and can reduce the risk of flooding by giving storm water room to spread. Property values along restored rivers often rise. Critics, however, note that some flagship projects rely on water pumped in artificially, making them closer to elaborate fountains than to genuine ecosystems, and that the resulting increase in property prices can displace the small traders who once occupied the area. Smaller, less glamorous projects may ultimately deliver more ecological value per pound spent.`,statements:[{text:`Critics say all daylighting projects are entirely natural ecosystems.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many urban rivers were covered over during industrialisation.`,answer:!0},{text:`The famous East Asian project involved building a new motorway over a stream.`,answer:!1},{text:`Restored rivers can help lower temperatures in nearby streets.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-091`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says shortlisted novels 'see sales multiply', judges read over a hundred books 'in a matter of months', a moderately admired book 'can triumph' over a divisive one, and prizes favour 'earnest, issue-driven novels'.`,passage:`Literary prizes occupy a peculiar place in cultural life. Ostensibly designed to reward excellence, they also serve as powerful marketing tools: a novel shortlisted for a major award can see its sales multiply within days, and the eventual winner may sell more copies in a month than in all the years since its publication. Publishers, unsurprisingly, compete vigorously to have their titles considered. Yet the judging process is notoriously idiosyncratic. Panels typically consist of a handful of critics, writers and celebrities who must read well over a hundred books in a matter of months, and their final choice often reflects compromise rather than consensus. Former judges have admitted that a book everyone admired moderately can triumph over a more daring work that divided the panel. Critics of the system argue that prizes encourage a narrow conception of literary merit, favouring earnest, issue-driven novels over comic or genre fiction. Defenders respond that, whatever their flaws, prizes generate public conversation about books at a time when newspapers have drastically cut their review sections. For lesser-known authors in particular, even appearing on a longlist can provide a precious moment of visibility in a crowded and unforgiving market.`,statements:[{text:`Being shortlisted for a major prize can greatly increase a book's sales.`,answer:!0},{text:`Judges usually have several years to read the submitted books.`,answer:!1},{text:`A book that divides the judges may lose to one that everyone moderately likes.`,answer:!0},{text:`Critics claim that prizes tend to favour comic and genre fiction.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-092`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says many commit to one sport 'before the age of ten', elite athletes often 'specialised only in their mid-teens', specialisation is linked to 'overuse injuries', and changing parents' attitudes is 'a slower task'.`,passage:`Children's sport has become increasingly specialised. Where young people once moved between football in winter, cricket or athletics in summer and whatever games happened to be played in the street, many now commit to a single sport before the age of ten, training year-round under the guidance of specialist coaches. Parents are often motivated by the hope of a scholarship or a professional career, encouraged by the widely cited claim that mastery requires ten thousand hours of deliberate practice. Sports scientists are increasingly sceptical of this approach. Studies of elite athletes reveal that a large proportion played a variety of sports in childhood and specialised only in their mid-teens, suggesting that early diversity may build a broader range of physical skills. Early specialisation, by contrast, has been linked to higher rates of overuse injuries, such as stress fractures and damaged growth plates, and to burnout, with many children abandoning sport altogether by adolescence. Some national governing bodies have responded by discouraging intensive competition for younger age groups and by promoting multi-sport programmes. Changing parental attitudes, though, is a slower task, especially when the rewards of professional success are so lavishly displayed in the media.`,statements:[{text:`Parental attitudes have already changed quickly in response to the research.`,answer:!1},{text:`Many children now focus on a single sport from a young age.`,answer:!0},{text:`Early specialisation has been associated with more overuse injuries.`,answer:!0},{text:`Studies show that most elite athletes specialised before the age of ten.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-093`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says platforms aimed to use resources 'that would otherwise lie idle', listings are 'dominated' by professional operators, some cities 'cap the number of nights', and enforcement is 'the persistent difficulty'.`,passage:`The notion of a 'sharing economy' was once synonymous with idealism. Early platforms promised to let neighbours lend each other drills, rent out spare rooms to travellers and share car journeys, making more efficient use of resources that would otherwise lie idle. Some of these services have delivered on that promise, but the largest have evolved into something rather different. Short-term rental sites, for instance, are now dominated in many cities not by residents letting a spare room occasionally but by professional operators managing dozens of entire apartments. Housing campaigners argue that this has removed thousands of homes from the long-term rental market, pushing up rents for local people and hollowing out historic centres that now feel more like hotels than neighbourhoods. City authorities have responded with a patchwork of measures: some cap the number of nights a property can be let each year, others require hosts to register with the council, and a few have banned entire-home rentals in certain districts. Enforcement is the persistent difficulty, since listings can be created and removed far faster than inspectors can check them. The episode illustrates how a technology marketed as communal can, once scaled up, take on all the characteristics of a conventional industry.`,statements:[{text:`Enforcing the new rules has proved easy for city authorities.`,answer:!1},{text:`Most listings in many cities still come from residents renting out a spare room.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some cities limit how many nights a year a property can be rented out.`,answer:!0},{text:`Early sharing platforms were associated with making better use of unused resources.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-094`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says richer individuals 'reported greater life satisfaction', average happiness 'had barely risen', later research found satisfaction rises with income but each pound brings 'a smaller increase', and 'several countries now publish' wellbeing statistics.`,passage:`The relationship between happiness and income has fascinated economists ever since a celebrated study in the 1970s observed that, although richer individuals within a country reported greater life satisfaction than poorer ones, average happiness in wealthy nations had barely risen over decades of economic growth. This apparent contradiction became known as a paradox, and it was widely interpreted as evidence that once basic needs are met, further growth does little for wellbeing. Subsequent research has muddied the picture. With access to far larger international datasets, some economists have found that life satisfaction does continue to rise with income, even among the wealthy, though each additional pound brings a smaller increase than the last. Others maintain that relative position matters more than absolute wealth: people judge their circumstances largely by comparison with their neighbours, so when everyone becomes richer together, nobody feels much better off. The policy implications are significant. If comparison dominates, then growth may be a poor guide to progress, and governments might do better to focus on reducing inequality or improving public services. Several countries now publish official wellbeing statistics alongside conventional measures such as gross domestic product.`,statements:[{text:`The 1970s study found that richer people within a country tended to be more satisfied with life.`,answer:!0},{text:`According to that study, happiness in wealthy nations rose sharply alongside economic growth.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some later research suggests that satisfaction keeps rising with income, though more slowly.`,answer:!0},{text:`No country has yet begun publishing official wellbeing figures.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-095`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says vocational routes are presented as 'a fallback', many technical apprentices 'earn more than graduates' in some subjects, employers 'take an active role in designing the curriculum', and some firms used levies to 'rebrand existing management courses'.`,passage:`Apprenticeships have long suffered from an image problem in countries where a university degree is regarded as the natural destination for capable school-leavers. Parents and teachers often present vocational routes as a fallback for those who are not academically inclined, despite evidence that many apprentices in technical fields go on to earn more than graduates in some arts and humanities subjects. In parts of continental Europe, by contrast, apprenticeships enjoy considerable prestige. Young people split their time between a workplace and a vocational college, employers take an active role in designing the curriculum, and qualifications are recognised across entire industries. Countries operating such systems tend to have relatively low youth unemployment, although economists caution that this may also reflect other features of their labour markets. Attempts to transplant the model elsewhere have met with mixed results. Governments have introduced levies requiring large employers to fund training, but some firms have used the money to rebrand existing management courses as apprenticeships rather than to create new opportunities for young people. Without a deep-rooted culture of employer involvement, reformers have discovered, it is difficult to replicate in a few years an institution that elsewhere took generations to mature.`,statements:[{text:`In some countries, apprenticeships are often seen as a second-best option.`,answer:!0},{text:`In parts of continental Europe, employers help to design apprenticeship courses.`,answer:!0},{text:`Apprentices in technical fields always earn less than university graduates.`,answer:!1},{text:`Training levies have always been used to create new opportunities for young people.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-096`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says beloved monuments 'were derided as eyesores', hostility is 'amplified by social media', bodies are 'increasingly consulting local communities', and some artists fear consultation produces 'bland, inoffensive pieces'.`,passage:`Public art has a habit of provoking outrage before it provokes affection. Some of the most beloved monuments in the world were derided as eyesores when they were first unveiled, attacked by critics and residents alike for their cost, their scale or their perceived ugliness. Over time, familiarity worked its quiet magic, and the structures came to be regarded as emblems of the cities they once supposedly disfigured. Contemporary commissions face similar hostility, often amplified by social media, where a single unflattering photograph can turn a sculpture into a national joke within hours. Commissioning bodies have therefore become more cautious, increasingly consulting local communities before a work is chosen. Advocates of this approach argue that art installed in shared spaces should reflect the views of those who must live with it every day. Some artists, however, fear that consultation can degenerate into design by committee, producing bland, inoffensive pieces that nobody hates and nobody loves. They point out that a public vote would almost certainly have rejected several of the works now celebrated as masterpieces. Striking the balance between democratic accountability and artistic ambition is, as one curator observed, a problem without a formula.`,statements:[{text:`Social media has reduced the criticism faced by new public artworks.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some famous monuments were unpopular when they were first built.`,answer:!0},{text:`The artists mentioned believe consultation always leads to bold and exciting art.`,answer:!1},{text:`Commissioning bodies now often consult local residents before choosing a work.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-097`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says early tests found 'only the crudest substances', the passport looks for 'unexplained fluctuations' rather than a specific substance, some athletes 'never actually failed a conventional test', and confidence 'remains stubbornly low'.`,passage:`Doping scandals have tarnished almost every major sport, and the methods used to evade detection have grown steadily more sophisticated. Early tests could identify only the crudest substances, and athletes soon learned to stop taking drugs shortly before competitions so that traces would disappear in time. In response, anti-doping authorities introduced out-of-competition testing, requiring elite athletes to declare their whereabouts every day so that inspectors could arrive unannounced. A further innovation was the biological passport, which tracks an athlete's blood and hormone levels over time. Rather than searching for a specific forbidden substance, investigators look for unexplained fluctuations that suggest manipulation. This approach has led to sanctions against athletes who never actually failed a conventional test. It has also raised concerns about fairness, since natural variations caused by illness, altitude training or genetics can occasionally resemble the effects of doping. Athletes' representatives complain that the whereabouts system intrudes deeply into private life, and that a missed test caused by a genuine mistake can carry the same penalty as deliberate evasion. Authorities insist that such intrusions are the price of credible competition, though public confidence in the cleanliness of elite sport remains stubbornly low.`,statements:[{text:`Early drug tests could detect only the most obvious substances.`,answer:!0},{text:`The biological passport searches mainly for one specific banned drug.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some athletes have been punished without failing a standard drug test.`,answer:!0},{text:`Public confidence that elite sport is clean has risen sharply.`,answer:!1}]},{id:`c2-tf-098`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says losses in poorer nations occur 'early in the supply chain', in rich countries the largest share is discarded 'by households and retailers', 'best before' 'merely indicates quality', and laws oblige retailers 'to donate unsold edible food'.`,passage:`Food waste is one of the more embarrassing paradoxes of affluent societies. Estimates suggest that roughly a third of all food produced for human consumption is lost or thrown away, at the same time as millions of people struggle to afford an adequate diet. The causes vary with a country's level of development. In poorer nations, most losses occur early in the supply chain, as crops spoil in fields or during storage and transport because of inadequate refrigeration and infrastructure. In wealthy countries, by contrast, the largest share is discarded by households and retailers. Confusion over date labels is a significant factor: many consumers throw away food that is still perfectly safe because they do not understand the difference between a 'use by' date, which concerns safety, and a 'best before' date, which merely indicates quality. Supermarkets have also been criticised for rejecting fruit and vegetables that fail to meet strict cosmetic standards. Several countries have introduced laws obliging large retailers to donate unsold edible food to charities rather than destroy it, and some chains now sell misshapen produce at a discount. Campaigners welcome these steps but argue that lasting change will require a broader shift in how consumers value food.`,statements:[{text:`In poorer countries, much food is lost before it reaches consumers.`,answer:!0},{text:`In wealthy countries, most waste occurs on farms rather than in homes and shops.`,answer:!1},{text:`A 'best before' date indicates when food becomes unsafe to eat.`,answer:!1},{text:`Some laws require large shops to give unsold edible food to charities.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-099`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says the burden 'lies on the state, not on the accused', online commentary makes an impartial jury 'increasingly difficult' to find, courts have 'in rare cases' abandoned trials, and reverse burdens require defendants 'to establish certain facts'.`,passage:`The presumption of innocence is among the most venerable principles of criminal justice. It holds that a defendant must be treated as innocent until the prosecution has proved guilt, usually to the demanding standard of 'beyond reasonable doubt'. The burden therefore lies on the state, not on the accused, who is under no obligation to prove anything at all. In practice, the principle faces mounting pressure. Media coverage of high-profile arrests can create an impression of guilt long before any trial, and the spread of commentary online makes it increasingly difficult to assemble a jury untouched by prejudicial material. Courts have responded by imposing reporting restrictions and, in rare cases, by abandoning trials altogether. Some legislatures have also introduced so-called reverse burdens for particular offences, requiring defendants to establish certain facts, such as possessing a lawful excuse for carrying a weapon. Defenders of these provisions argue that the facts concerned are peculiarly within the defendant's knowledge, so it is reasonable to expect them to provide an explanation. Civil liberties groups counter that each exception chips away at a fundamental safeguard, and that a principle riddled with exceptions risks becoming little more than a slogan.`,statements:[{text:`Under the presumption of innocence, the accused must prove they did not commit the crime.`,answer:!1},{text:`Media coverage can make it harder to find an unbiased jury.`,answer:!0},{text:`Courts have never stopped a trial because of prejudicial publicity.`,answer:!1},{text:`Reverse burdens require defendants to establish certain facts for some offences.`,answer:!0}]},{id:`c2-tf-100`,type:`true-false`,level:`C2`,topic:`reading`,prompt:`Read the text and decide if each sentence is true or false.`,explanation:`The text says life expectancy after retirement was 'often just a few years', the number of workers per retiree is 'steadily falling', raising the age provoked 'strikes and protests', and such schemes 'add considerable administrative complexity'.`,passage:`Ageing populations are forcing governments across the developed world to rethink the economics of retirement. When state pension systems were designed, life expectancy after retirement was often just a few years, and each pensioner was supported by the contributions of many workers. Today people routinely live into their eighties and beyond, while lower birth rates mean that the number of workers for every retiree is steadily falling. The arithmetic is unforgiving. Governments can raise taxes or contributions, reduce the generosity of pensions, increase the retirement age, or encourage immigration to boost the working-age population, and most have adopted some combination of these options. Raising the pension age is often the most controversial measure, provoking strikes and protests in several countries. Opponents point out that gains in life expectancy have been unevenly distributed: manual workers and those in poorer regions tend to die younger and to suffer ill health earlier, so a uniform increase in the retirement age penalises them disproportionately. Some economists have proposed linking pension ages to occupation or to years of contributions rather than to age alone, though such schemes add considerable administrative complexity and can be difficult to explain to the public.`,statements:[{text:`Proposals to link pension age to occupation are described as simple to administer.`,answer:!1},{text:`The number of workers supporting each retiree is increasing.`,answer:!1},{text:`Raising the retirement age has led to protests in several countries.`,answer:!0},{text:`When pension systems were created, people usually lived only a few years after retiring.`,answer:!0}]}]};export{e as default};